ies/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet-3","page_id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet#what-a-correct-reader-returns","content":"What a correct reader returns"},"1902":{"id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet-4","page_id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet#check-it-yourself","content":"Check it yourself"},"1903":{"id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet-5","page_id":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/entries/upgrade-protocol-30-testnet#check-it-yourself","content":"Entry id `upgrade.protocol-30-testnet` · compiled from `catalog/upgrade.protocol-30-testnet.json` · `entry.json`"},"1904":{"id":"/docs/corpus","page_id":"/docs/corpus","type":"page","content":"The corpus","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Corpus"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus"},"1905":{"id":"/docs/corpus-0","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus","content":"Rare but valid Stellar ledger data, as bytes stellar-core actually emitted, each entry tagged with its CAP and the values a correct reader must return."},"1906":{"id":"/docs/corpus-1","page_id":"/docs/corpus","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus#how-an-entry-is-built","content":"How an entry is built"},"1907":{"id":"/docs/corpus-2","page_id":"/docs/corpus","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus#picked-from-the-hazard-registry","content":"Picked from the hazard registry"},"1908":{"id":"/docs/corpus-3","page_id":"/docs/corpus","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus#written-in-the-catalog-compiled-from-real-runs","content":"Written in the catalog, compiled from real runs"},"1909":{"id":"/docs/corpus-4","page_id":"/docs/corpus","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus#facts-and-what-backs-each-one","content":"Facts, and what backs each one"},"1910":{"id":"/docs/corpus-5","page_id":"/docs/corpus","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus#verified-on-every-change","content":"Verified on every change"},"1911":{"id":"/docs/corpus-6","page_id":"/docs/corpus","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus#files","content":"Files"},"1912":{"id":"/docs/corpus-7","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus","content":"**What it is.** The heart of XDRProof: real Stellar ledgers in their rarest valid shapes, each kept as the exact bytes stellar-core wrote, with the answers a correct reader must give."},"1913":{"id":"/docs/corpus-8","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus","content":"**Why it matters.** Your code meets ordinary ledgers every day and these rarely, often for the first time in production. The corpus lets you meet them in a test."},"1914":{"id":"/docs/corpus-9","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus","content":"The corpus is a set of **entries**, plus 37 mined ledgers from the real networks. Each entry is one rare shape, such as a signed-payload signer or a trustline created by a contract call, kept as real `LedgerCloseMeta` bytes. Each entry also carries the facts a correct reader must find in those bytes."},"1915":{"id":"/docs/corpus-10","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus","content":"Entries grow by purpose, not by volume. Each one names the hazards it targets, and where in the XDR they sit."},"1916":{"id":"/docs/corpus-11","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus","content":"The full list, grouped by family, is on All entries; every entry has its own page."},"1917":{"id":"/docs/corpus-12","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#picked-from-the-hazard-registry","content":"A hazard is a named way readers break, with its evidence: an upstream issue, a protocol rule or a measurement. An entry targets one or more hazards, and says where each sits in the XDR (its `placement`)."},"1918":{"id":"/docs/corpus-13","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#written-in-the-catalog-compiled-from-real-runs","content":"The entry's intent and facts are written in its catalog file. The compiler takes the bytes from a recipe run of a bare standalone stellar-core, or from a ledger **mined** from public history, and resolves every fact against them. A fact that doesn't hold fails the build. `make generate` re-runs every recipe twice from scratch and checks that every shipped generated variant is reproduced: the same bytes, or the same content in another change order."},"1919":{"id":"/docs/corpus-14","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#facts-and-what-backs-each-one","content":"Every expected value says what backs it: `construction` (the recipe set that value), `snapshot` (the SEP-0051 rendering by the pinned stellar-xdr crate), `core` (stellar-core's own decoder), `walker` (XDRProof's schema walker) or `review`.  of the  facts are also confirmed by both stellar-core's decoder and the schema walker; the **Backed by** column on each entry page shows exactly which."},"1920":{"id":"/docs/corpus-15","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#verified-on-every-change","content":"`make verify` checks the schema, sizes and hashes, a byte-for-byte round trip through native rs-stellar-xdr, the focus transaction and every fact. `make oracle` checks that each fact's values also appear in what stellar-core's own decoder and the schema walker read."},"1921":{"id":"/docs/corpus-16","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#files","content":"<File name=\"<id>.json\" />"},"1922":{"id":"/docs/corpus-17","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#files","content":"<File name=\"entry.json\" />"},"1923":{"id":"/docs/corpus-18","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#files","content":"<File name=\"p30-mainnet-generated.xdr\" />"},"1924":{"id":"/docs/corpus-19","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#files","content":"<File name=\"p28-testnet-generated.xdr\" />"},"1925":{"id":"/docs/corpus-20","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#files","content":"<File name=\"manifest.json\" />"},"1926":{"id":"/docs/corpus-21","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#files","content":"<File name=\"entry.schema.json\" />"},"1927":{"id":"/docs/corpus-22","page_id":"/docs/corpus","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus#files","content":"`manifest.json` is generated from the entries, one row per variant. `make verify` fails if it is out of date."},"1928":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","type":"page","content":"Mined ledgers","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Corpus","Real network data"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/mined"},"1929":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-0","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"37 real mainnet and testnet ledgers from the public data lake, for shapes a standalone generator cannot produce, or that are worth having as real network bytes: eviction at the cap, settings votes, a mainnet freeze, protocol-12 V0 envelopes."},"1930":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-1","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/mined#every-mined-shape","content":"Every mined shape"},"1931":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-2","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"**What it is.** Real mainnet and testnet ledgers, downloaded from the public data lake with their hashes and the query that found them."},"1932":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-3","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"**Why it matters.** Some shapes only happen on a live network, such as eviction at the per-ledger cap or a validator vote. These are the real bytes, not a reconstruction."},"1933":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-4","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"Some shapes only happen on a real network, and some are worth having as real network bytes. Examples:"},"1934":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-5","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"eviction at the per-ledger cap;"},"1935":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-6","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"validator settings votes;"},"1936":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-7","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"a mainnet freeze of ledger keys (CAP-0077);"},"1937":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-8","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"protocol-12 V0 envelopes;"},"1938":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-9","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"protocol-22 refunds;"},"1939":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-10","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"multi-stage parallel Soroban phases."},"1940":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-11","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"`mined/` holds those as real `LedgerCloseMeta`, fetched from SDF's public data lake and located with BigQuery over the public Hubble dataset. The queries are in `mined/queries/`."},"1941":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-12","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"| File                                        | What it is                                                                                                                       |\n| ------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `mined/ledgers/<network>-<seq>.lcm.xdr.zst` | One ledger, zstd-compressed. Its sha256 and size describe the decompressed bytes: the lake object minus its 12-byte batch header |","table":"table-0"},"1942":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-13","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"| File               | What it is                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `mined/index.json` | One record per shape found in a ledger: the lake URL, the stellar-core version that wrote it, how it was located, the findings with exact JSON paths, and why readers break on it |","table":"table-0"},"1943":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-14","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"`make verify` also runs `tools/mined-verify.mjs`. Every file must match its hash, round-trip byte for byte through native rs-stellar-xdr, and carry the recorded sequence and protocol. Every finding's transaction and JSON path must be present. `make coverage` reports the union arms these ledgers reach: ** of **."},"1944":{"id":"/docs/corpus/mined-15","page_id":"/docs/corpus/mined","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/mined","content":"<Callout title=\"Network data, not entries\">These ledgers were written by SDF's Galexie with its default flags, so their metadata differs from a default node's (see Found while building). Most are not corpus entries: entries carry facts and hazards, and these carry findings. The catalog also compiles entries from them: entries so far, built from of these ledgers, the signed-payload signer among them.</Callout>"},"1945":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","type":"page","content":"Recipes","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Corpus","Making entries"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/recipes"},"1946":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-0","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"Deterministic scripts that drive a bare standalone stellar-core to emit rare shapes: fixed seeds, fixed close times, no RPC, no network, no funds."},"1947":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-1","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#the-two-recipe-sets","content":"The two recipe sets"},"1948":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-2","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#the-test-contracts","content":"The test contracts"},"1949":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-3","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#how-they-are-run","content":"How they are run"},"1950":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-4","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"**What it is.** Small scripts that make a private, offline stellar-core produce each rare shape on demand."},"1951":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-5","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"**Why it matters.** A shape you can rebuild from scratch is one anyone can verify, and one that can be made again when the protocol changes."},"1952":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-6","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"A recipe is a Node script (using `@stellar/stellar-sdk` 17.4.0) that drives one fresh **standalone stellar-core** over its HTTP port:"},"1953":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-7","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"**Fixed seeds.** Every keypair is `sha256('xdrproof:' + name)`; the root account comes from the passphrase."},"1954":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-8","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"**Fixed time.** Ledger n closes at `1767225600 + n × 30 days`."},"1955":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-9","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"**No RPC, no simulation.** Transactions go straight to core's `tx` endpoint, and ledgers close with `manualclose`."},"1956":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-10","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"**Ledger 2 is always the protocol upgrade,** and never an entry."},"1957":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-11","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes","content":"Core's metadata stream is split into one `ledger-NNN.xdr` file per ledger, plus `SHA256SUMS`. An entry is one of those ledgers, chosen because it holds a rare shape."},"1958":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-12","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#the-two-recipe-sets","content":"| Set     | Script                    | Ledgers               | What it covers                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| ------- | ------------------------- | --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Classic | `recipes/classic/gen.mjs` | 3–105, at protocol 30 | Basics (the signed-payload signers at ledger 6), a catalogue of operations, two failure suites, blueprint placements, maximum-length vectors, a revocation cascade, surge pricing, and a final base-fee, set-size and ledger-flags upgrade |","table":"table-0"},"1959":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-13","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#the-two-recipe-sets","content":"| Set     | Script                        | Ledgers     | What it covers                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| ------- | ----------------------------- | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Soroban | `recipes/soroban/gen/gen.mjs` | 3–94 at P30 | Blueprint placements, apply-time failures, fee-bump inner failures, forged and V2-rendered auth signatures, two test contracts, delegated custom accounts, external-ref executables, config upgrades, frozen keys, eviction and automatic restore |","table":"table-0"},"1960":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-14","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#the-two-recipe-sets","content":"Entries are compiled from recipe steps (and mined ledgers) by the catalog:  so far. The hazard registry references recipe ledgers by number."},"1961":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-15","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#the-test-contracts","content":"| Contract       | SDK                | What it does                                                                                        |\n| -------------- | ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `xdrproof-kit` | soroban-sdk 25.3.0 | `put(k, v)` stores a value, emits it and returns it; `nest` builds authorization trees of any depth |","table":"table-1"},"1962":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-16","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#the-test-contracts","content":"| Contract        | SDK                | What it does                                                                                                                              |\n| --------------- | ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `xdrproof-kit2` | soroban-sdk 28.0.0 | A constructor, persistent, temporary and instance storage, CAP-0085 executable references, and a `__check_auth` that delegates (CAP-0071) |","table":"table-1"},"1963":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-17","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#the-test-contracts","content":"Both ship as prebuilt Wasm next to their source."},"1964":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-18","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#how-they-are-run","content":"Each run starts the `stellar/quickstart` image pinned by digest in `recipes/core-image`, and refuses anything but stellar-core v30.0.0. The core config sets manual close, a fixed node seed, and one **emitter-flag profile** from `recipes/flags/`:"},"1965":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-19","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#how-they-are-run","content":"| Profile | Flags set                                                                                      | Who runs this way             |\n| ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `base`  | `EMIT_CLASSIC_EVENTS`, `EMIT_SOROBAN_TRANSACTION_META_EXT_V1`, `EMIT_LEDGER_CLOSE_META_EXT_V1` | the corpus's shipped variants |","table":"table-2"},"1966":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-20","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#how-they-are-run","content":"| Profile   | Flags set                                                                                                                  | Who runs this way                                                  |\n| --------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `galexie` | all six: the three above plus `BACKFILL_STELLAR_ASSET_EVENTS`, `BACKFILL_RESTORE_META`, `ENABLE_SOROBAN_DIAGNOSTIC_EVENTS` | Galexie's default config, which public data lakes are written with |","table":"table-2"},"1967":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-21","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#how-they-are-run","content":"| Profile | Flags set | Who runs this way                       |\n| ------- | --------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| `bare`  | none      | a node on stellar-core's example config |","table":"table-2"},"1968":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-22","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#how-they-are-run","content":"Soroban runs also set `TESTING_SOROBAN_HIGH_LIMIT_OVERRIDE`. Variant keys carry the profile as a suffix when it is not `base`."},"1969":{"id":"/docs/corpus/recipes-23","page_id":"/docs/corpus/recipes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/recipes#how-they-are-run","content":"`make generate` runs 10 configurations twice each: classic P30 on both passphrases, Soroban P30 and P28 on both passphrases, and the galexie and bare profiles at P30 mainnet for each lane. It checks that every shipped generated variant is reproduced. See Generate."},"1970":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","type":"page","content":"Manifest schema","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Corpus"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/schema"},"1971":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-0","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema","content":"Every field of an entry, generated from entry.schema.json (JSON Schema 2020-12), with how the RFP's required fields map onto it."},"1972":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-1","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/schema#the-rfps-fields","content":"The RFP's fields"},"1973":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-2","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/schema#entry","content":"Entry"},"1974":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-3","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/schema#variant","content":"Variant"},"1975":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-4","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/schema#fact","content":"Fact"},"1976":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-5","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/corpus/schema#sdk-check","content":"SDK check"},"1977":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-6","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema","content":"**What it is.** The format of an entry, field by field, as a JSON Schema."},"1978":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-7","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema","content":"**Why it matters.** Any tool, in any language, can load the corpus and know what each field means without reading XDRProof's code."},"1979":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-8","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema","content":"The schema is `schema/v1/entry.schema.json`, version 1.1. Every table on this page is generated from it."},"1980":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-9","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#the-rfps-fields","content":"| RFP field | Schema                                            |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\n| Entry ID  | `id` + `variants[].key` (permanent; never reused) |","table":"table-0"},"1981":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-10","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#the-rfps-fields","content":"| RFP field | Schema |\n| --------- | ------ |\n| CAP       | `caps` |","table":"table-0"},"1982":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-11","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#the-rfps-fields","content":"| RFP field        | Schema                |\n| ---------------- | --------------------- |\n| Protocol version | `variants[].protocol` |","table":"table-0"},"1983":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-12","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#the-rfps-fields","content":"| RFP field | Schema                                                                                                                    |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Source    | `variants[].source`: `mined` with the lake URL and sha256, or `generated` with the core version, emitter flags and recipe |","table":"table-0"},"1984":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-13","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#the-rfps-fields","content":"| RFP field      | Schema                                              |\n| -------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| Synthetic flag | `variants[].synthetic` (with `emittable` when true) |","table":"table-0"},"1985":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-14","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#the-rfps-fields","content":"| RFP field               | Schema                                                                      |\n| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Expected decoded values | `expected.facts` (order-independent where needed) and `expected.sdk_checks` |","table":"table-0"},"1986":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-15","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#the-rfps-fields","content":"Beyond those, each entry names its `hazards` (with the `placement` in the XDR), and each variant its `focus` transaction. Variants may be zstd-compressed (large mainnet ledgers); `size` and `sha256` describe the decompressed bytes."},"1987":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-16","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#variant","content":"One ledger file of an entry: a protocol, a network passphrase, and how it was obtained."},"1988":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-17","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#fact","content":"One value a correct reader must find, at a path in the SEP-0051 JSON rendering. It is given as `equals`, `length` or `some` (a change that matches, in any order)."},"1989":{"id":"/docs/corpus/schema-18","page_id":"/docs/corpus/schema","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/corpus/schema#sdk-check","content":"A rule for an SDK helper rather than a value. For example, a native projection must keep every entry of a map or refuse it."},"1990":{"id":"/docs/findings","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"page","content":"Found while building","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Findings"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings"},"1991":{"id":"/docs/findings-0","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings","content":"What building the corpus measured about stellar-core, its metadata and the decoders. Each one is a hazard for indexers."},"1992":{"id":"/docs/findings-1","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#stellar-core-emits-some-operation-changes-in-a-random-order","content":"stellar-core emits some operation changes in a random order"},"1993":{"id":"/docs/findings-2","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#node-flags-change-the-metadata-never-the-ledger","content":"Node flags change the metadata, never the ledger"},"1994":{"id":"/docs/findings-3","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#a-soroban-ledger-written-with-diagnostic-events-on-is-not-byte-reproducible","content":"A Soroban ledger written with diagnostic events on is not byte-reproducible"},"1995":{"id":"/docs/findings-4","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#the-public-data-lake-carries-transactionmeta-v4-even-for-2019-ledgers","content":"The public data lake carries TransactionMeta v4, even for 2019 ledgers"},"1996":{"id":"/docs/findings-5","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#a-soroban-fee-source-merged-earlier-in-the-same-ledger-gets-no-refund","content":"A Soroban fee source merged earlier in the same ledger gets no refund"},"1997":{"id":"/docs/findings-6","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#stellar-cores-own-golden-ledgers-reach-254-of-499-arms","content":"stellar-core's own golden ledgers reach 254 of 499 arms"},"1998":{"id":"/docs/findings-7","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#soroban-authorization-trees-apply-up-to-245-levels-deep","content":"Soroban authorization trees apply up to 245 levels deep"},"1999":{"id":"/docs/findings-8","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#the-wasm-build-of-rs-stellar-xdr-cannot-decode-those-ledgers","content":"The WASM build of rs-stellar-xdr cannot decode those ledgers"},"2000":{"id":"/docs/findings-9","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#a-create-contract-authorization-is-signed-over-bytes-it-does-not-contain","content":"A create-contract authorization is signed over bytes it does not contain"},"2001":{"id":"/docs/findings-10","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#codes-read-as-submission-only-appear-in-closed-ledgers","content":"Codes read as submission-only appear in closed ledgers"},"2002":{"id":"/docs/findings-11","page_id":"/docs/findings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings#order-across-ledger-keys-is-unstable-the-pairing-for-one-key-is-stable","content":"Order across ledger keys is unstable; the pairing for one key is stable"},"2003":{"id":"/docs/findings-12","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings","content":"**What it is.** What building the corpus measured about stellar-core and the decoders: things the documentation doesn't say."},"2004":{"id":"/docs/findings-13","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings","content":"**Why it matters.** Each one is a trap for anyone reading the ledger, and each is now a test in the corpus."},"2005":{"id":"/docs/findings-14","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings","content":"Each finding says where it comes from: a committed measurement, a mined ledger, or an observation on recipe runs. The recipe runs themselves are not shipped. The recipes are, along with the order-scan and flag-profile results."},"2006":{"id":"/docs/findings-15","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#stellar-core-emits-some-operation-changes-in-a-random-order","content":"Across  fresh runs of each recipe lane,  of  classic recipe ledgers and  of  Soroban ones came out in more than one byte order (the protocol-upgrade ledger aside, which varies too). Their headers and content were identical.  classic ledgers () had a different order in every run. The cause is hash-map iteration with a per-process random salt. Measured by `make order-scan`; the results are committed in `recipes/*/order-scan/`."},"2007":{"id":"/docs/findings-16","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#stellar-core-emits-some-operation-changes-in-a-random-order","content":"<Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"For indexers\">Never depend on the order of changes within an operation.</Callout>"},"2008":{"id":"/docs/findings-17","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#node-flags-change-the-metadata-never-the-ledger","content":"The recipes were run under the three flag profiles (base, Galexie and bare). Every ledger of both lanes keeps the same header hash."},"2009":{"id":"/docs/findings-18","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#node-flags-change-the-metadata-never-the-ledger","content":"**Bare** changes the metadata content of every ledger except the protocol-upgrade one. There are no classic events, no fee events, and v0 meta extensions."},"2010":{"id":"/docs/findings-19","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#node-flags-change-the-metadata-never-the-ledger","content":"**Galexie's** extra flags change no classic ledger, and 46 Soroban ones: every ledger with a contract call. In those, each contract event appears twice, in the operation's events and again among the diagnostic events."},"2011":{"id":"/docs/findings-20","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#node-flags-change-the-metadata-never-the-ledger","content":"`make generate` re-measures this into `recipes/<lane>/profiles/`."},"2012":{"id":"/docs/findings-21","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#a-soroban-ledger-written-with-diagnostic-events-on-is-not-byte-reproducible","content":"Every call carries a `core_metrics` diagnostic event, `invoke_time_nsecs`: the wall-clock time the host spent. Two nodes running Galexie's default config write different bytes for the same ledger. The corpus's normalised form zeroes that one value."},"2013":{"id":"/docs/findings-22","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#the-public-data-lake-carries-transactionmeta-v4-even-for-2019-ledgers","content":"Every lake object inspected, at protocols 12, 22 and 29, has v4 meta. The protocol-12 mainnet ledger in `mined/` has v4 meta and 532 V0 envelopes. This is because Galexie sets `BACKFILL_STELLAR_ASSET_EVENTS`, which makes stellar-core write v4 at any protocol. A default node replaying the same ledger writes v2 (v3 for protocols 20–22). Only the `LedgerCloseMeta` arm varies by era: v0 at protocol 12, v1 at 22, v2 at 29."},"2014":{"id":"/docs/findings-23","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#a-soroban-fee-source-merged-earlier-in-the-same-ledger-gets-no-refund","content":"`fee_charged` is then the full declared maximum, with no refund change and no refund event. A reader that computes the fee as the charge minus the refund, or that expects a refund for every Soroban transaction, gets it wrong."},"2015":{"id":"/docs/findings-24","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#stellar-cores-own-golden-ledgers-reach-254-of-499-arms","content":"A `LedgerCloseMeta` reader can meet 499 XDR union arms at v30.0. stellar-core's own golden ledger files reach 254 of them. For comparison, the corpus entries reach  and the mined ledgers  (Coverage). Many of the missing arms are result codes that never appear in a closed ledger. Measured by `make coverage-goldens` against stellar-core commit `f2b91947`."},"2016":{"id":"/docs/findings-25","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#soroban-authorization-trees-apply-up-to-245-levels-deep","content":"At Protocol 30, an authorization tree 245 levels deep applies. At 246 the transaction is included, but traps. Any indexer cap below about 246 meets real, valid ledgers. Re-checked by `make verify` on the entries `auth.tree-depth-245-max` and `auth.tree-depth-246-trapped`."},"2017":{"id":"/docs/findings-26","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#the-wasm-build-of-rs-stellar-xdr-cannot-decode-those-ledgers","content":"`@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0, rs-stellar-xdr compiled to WASM, refuses  corpus variants that stellar-core wrote (`depth limit exceeded`): 8 whose authorization tree is 245 levels deep, the deepest that applies, and 5 at 246, which core includes and traps. The package hard-codes `XDR_MAX_DEPTH = 500`, set to mirror the Soroban host's budget limit, and applies it to the whole `LedgerCloseMeta`, whose path down to the auth tree is deeper. js-stellar-sdk 17.4.0, go-stellar-sdk v0.9.0, both Python decoders and native rs-stellar-xdr read them. This is finding F-1, reported as js-stellar-xdr-json#67; see Reported upstream."},"2018":{"id":"/docs/findings-27","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#a-create-contract-authorization-is-signed-over-bytes-it-does-not-contain","content":"A valid v1 create-contract authorization is signed over its V2 rendering. The host reads `SOROBAN_AUTHORIZED_FUNCTION_TYPE_CREATE_CONTRACT_HOST_FN` into its V2 form and hashes the signature payload from the `CREATE_CONTRACT_V2_HOST_FN` rendering (soroban-env-host `auth.rs`, as stellar-core v30 links it at protocols 28 and 30). Signed over its own bytes, as js-stellar-base's `authorizeEntry` signs it, the entry fails ed25519 verification; signed over the V2 rendering, the contract is created."},"2019":{"id":"/docs/findings-28","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#a-create-contract-authorization-is-signed-over-bytes-it-does-not-contain","content":"This is by design since protocol 22, to keep one valid payload per action (stellar discussion #1501). Transactions built through RPC simulation get it right; offline signers must sign the V2 rendering. A verifier that recomputes the payload from the entry rejects a signature core accepted (hazard H-B16). `tools/auth-payload-check.mjs` re-checks it with hand-encoded preimages, no SDK."},"2020":{"id":"/docs/findings-29","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#codes-read-as-submission-only-appear-in-closed-ledgers","content":"Core re-checks sequence numbers, signatures and preconditions at apply. So a classic transaction earlier in the same ledger can make a queued Soroban transaction fail with `txBAD_AUTH_EXTRA` (a signature needed at submission becomes surplus once a master weight rises), and fee-bump inner transactions fail with `txBAD_SEQ`, `txNO_ACCOUNT`, `txBAD_AUTH`, `txBAD_AUTH_EXTRA` or `txBAD_MIN_SEQ_AGE_OR_GAP` inside `txFEE_BUMP_INNER_FAILED`. The corpus's failed-transaction family holds them."},"2021":{"id":"/docs/findings-30","page_id":"/docs/findings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings#order-across-ledger-keys-is-unstable-the-pairing-for-one-key-is-stable","content":"Between core processes, the order across different ledger keys changes. The STATE → UPDATED pairing for one key does not. Readers can rely on the pairing, never on the order between keys."},"2022":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","type":"page","content":"Reported upstream","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Findings"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reported"},"2023":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-0","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported","content":"Two bugs XDRProof's runs found that nobody had reported, now filed upstream with a figure and a reproduction: a lost refund in wallet-backend and a decoder that refuses a ledger stellar-core closed. A tested fix offered on an issue that was already open, evidence added to another, and where else each finding was checked."},"2024":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-1","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reported#f-3-balances-stored-short-on-protocol-2022-ledgers","content":"F-3: balances stored short on protocol 20–22 ledgers"},"2025":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-2","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reported#f-1-a-decoder-refuses-a-ledger-stellar-core-closed","content":"F-1: a decoder refuses a ledger stellar-core closed"},"2026":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-3","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reported#wallet-backend820-an-authorization-walk-with-no-limit","content":"wallet-backend#820: an authorization walk with no limit"},"2027":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-4","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reported#evidence-added-to-an-existing-report","content":"Evidence added to an existing report"},"2028":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-5","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reported#where-else-each-finding-was-checked","content":"Where else each finding was checked"},"2029":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-6","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reported#found-not-filed","content":"Found, not filed"},"2030":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-7","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported","content":"**What it is.** Two bugs XDRProof found in other projects' code that nobody had reported, filed upstream; a tested fix offered on an issue that was already open; and evidence added to another team's own report."},"2031":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-8","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported","content":"**Why it matters.** A test kit earns trust by what it catches. Each report below comes with a figure, a reproduction anyone can run, and, where we could, a tested fix. Filing is done the way the projects ask: a duplicate search first, one issue per bug."},"2032":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-9","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#f-3-balances-stored-short-on-protocol-2022-ledgers","content":"Before protocol 23, stellar-core credits a Soroban fee refund inside the transaction's own changes. wallet-backend builds native balances from fees, operations and the newer, separate refund block only, so every refunded account on such a ledger is stored short by exactly its refund, while wallet-backend's own balance history adds up."},"2033":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-10","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#f-3-balances-stored-short-on-protocol-2022-ledgers","content":"It bites when an indexer works through older ledgers, such as a catch-up from the data lake. The fix is on a branch; the whole corpus replayed into it handles both variants of that ledger and changes no other verdict."},"2034":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-11","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#f-1-a-decoder-refuses-a-ledger-stellar-core-closed","content":"`@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0, the WebAssembly build of rs-stellar-xdr, limits nesting to 500 levels for the whole value it decodes. stellar-core allows 500 per authorization entry, and the layers around a transaction add more, so the deepest trees core applies cannot be decoded once they are inside a transaction or a ledger. The corpus made them on a standalone stellar-core v30.0.0 with Soroban's resource limits raised for testing (`TESTING_SOROBAN_HIGH_LIMIT_OVERRIDE`), which the entry records."},"2035":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-12","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#f-1-a-decoder-refuses-a-ledger-stellar-core-closed","content":"It refuses  corpus variants that stellar-core wrote. Every other decoder tested reads them; see Conformance."},"2036":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-13","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#wallet-backend820-an-authorization-walk-with-no-limit","content":"wallet-backend walks the authorization tree a transaction declares with no depth or node limit (issue #820, filed by another team). The linter finds it as `XP002/walk-unbudgeted`."},"2037":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-14","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#wallet-backend820-an-authorization-walk-with-no-limit","content":"The fix offered bounds the walk well above anything the network applies today, and stops at the limit instead of failing. Our first version failed at depth 64, which would have halted ingestion on the 245-deep ledgers in the corpus; that mistake is now a linter rule of its own, `XP002/budget-fails`."},"2038":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-15","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#evidence-added-to-an-existing-report","content":"When one SDK fails a corpus entry, the same entry goes through the others. For F-1, java-stellar-sdk 6.0.0 refuses the same  ledgers at its default decoding depth; with a higher limit, every one decodes and round-trips."},"2039":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-16","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#evidence-added-to-an-existing-report","content":"Its maintainer had already opened #835 on 16 November, so this is not a new report: the ledgers and the depth each one needs were posted there as evidence for that issue's third point. The exact depths are in the table below."},"2040":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-17","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#where-else-each-finding-was-checked","content":"The sibling sweeps from `findings/REPORT.md`: **tested** means the entries were run through it; **inferred** means read from its code, not run."},"2041":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-18","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#found-not-filed","content":"| ID  | What                                                                                                     | Why not filed                                                                                |\n| --- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| F-2 | The Python SDK, `stellar-sdk` 17.0.1, raises on a muxed contract address (CAP-0084) when rendering JSON. | Already fixed on its main branch by the switch to `stellar-xdr`; `stellar-xdr` 0.2.1 passes. |","table":"table-0"},"2042":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-19","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#found-not-filed","content":"| ID  | What                                                                                                                  | Why not filed                                                                                                    |\n| --- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| F-4 | wallet-backend's Blend decoder uses an arbitrary `ScAddress` value as an account (found by `XP001/muxed-as-account`). | Not reachable today, and the Stellar contribution guide asks not to file speculative findings. The fix is ready. |","table":"table-0"},"2043":{"id":"/docs/findings/reported-20","page_id":"/docs/findings/reported","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reported#found-not-filed","content":"Bugs that would let anyone halt or crash an indexer are reported privately first, through SDF's security channel, and are not published here until a fix ships or 90 days pass (`findings/policy.md`). The full triage is `findings/REPORT.md`."},"2044":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","type":"page","content":"Upstream bugs","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Findings"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reproduced"},"2045":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-0","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced","content":"Real bugs in wallet-backend and the SDKs, reproduced from corpus ledgers and test vectors, plus what the runs found that nobody had reported. Each is pinned, so a fix upstream shows up as an unexpected pass."},"2046":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-1","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#wallet-backend-before-and-after-each-fix","content":"wallet-backend, before and after each fix"},"2047":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-2","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#wallet-backend-main-still-open","content":"wallet-backend main, still open"},"2048":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-3","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#found-by-xdrproof","content":"Found by XDRProof"},"2049":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-4","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"In the SDKs"},"2050":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-5","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced","content":"**What it is.** Real bugs that other teams found in wallet-backend and the SDKs, reproduced from corpus ledgers and test vectors."},"2051":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-6","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced","content":"**Why it matters.** Each one is pinned. If a fix regresses, or an old bug turns up in another SDK, the run fails."},"2052":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-7","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced","content":"Verdicts come from state, never from log text: the indexer's cursor, process and stored rows for replay, the value returned for conformance. Every row below is pinned in `replay/expectations/` or `conformance/expectations/`, so a fix upstream shows up as an unexpected pass and the row is updated. The full triage is findings/REPORT.md."},"2053":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-8","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#wallet-backend-before-and-after-each-fix","content":"Run by `make replay-sightings`: each fixed failure the corpus reproduces is replayed at the commits before its fix and at the fix. See Replay."},"2054":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-9","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#wallet-backend-main-still-open","content":"The whole corpus replayed into `main` ():  of  variants handled,  halted. The rest:"},"2055":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-10","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#found-by-xdrproof","content":"What the runs found that nobody had reported is on its own page, with figures and reproductions: Reported upstream."},"2056":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-11","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"Run by `make conformance`: the same corpus and vectors through every build. See Conformance."},"2057":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-12","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                           | What                                               | Upstream          |\n| ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| `js-stellar-sdk` 17.2.0, 17.2.1 | Assets ordered by strkey text instead of XDR bytes | , fixed in 17.2.1 |","table":"table-0"},"2058":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-13","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                   | What           | Upstream        |\n| ----------------------- | -------------- | --------------- |\n| `go-stellar-sdk` v0.7.1 | The #1766 twin | fixed in v0.7.2 |","table":"table-0"},"2059":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-14","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                           | What                                                                                 | Upstream          |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------- |\n| `js-stellar-sdk` 17.2.0, 17.2.1 | Muxed IDs and offer IDs accepted in non-canonical forms; offer IDs past 2^53 rounded | , fixed in 17.3.0 |","table":"table-0"},"2060":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-15","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                           | What                                                         | Upstream          |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------- |\n| `js-stellar-sdk` 17.2.0, 17.2.1 | Map keys that collide on native conversion, silently dropped | , fixed in 17.3.0 |","table":"table-0"},"2061":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-16","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                    | What                                                        | Upstream        |\n| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- | --------------- |\n| `py-stellar-base` 17.0.1 | `scval.to_native` keeps 2 of 5 colliding map keys, silently | , fixed on main |","table":"table-0"},"2062":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-17","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                    | What                                                                | Upstream          |\n| ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| `py-stellar-base` 17.0.1 | Accepts non-zero XDR padding, an optional flag of 2 and a bool of 2 | , , fixed on main |","table":"table-0"},"2063":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-18","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                    | What                                                                                                                                                                                            | Upstream        |\n| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------- |\n| `py-stellar-base` 17.0.1 | SEP-0051 JSON differs in four places (result codes, `ClawbackOp.from_`, `AllowTrustOp.asset`, `contract_id` as hex). With those four allowed, its JSON equals rs-stellar-xdr's on every variant | , fixed on main |","table":"table-0"},"2064":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-19","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                                    | What                                                                              | Upstream |\n| ---------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n| `rs-stellar-xdr` 30.0.0, native and WASM | A bool of 2 decodes as `false` and re-encodes as 0; stellar-core reads it as true | , open   |","table":"table-0"},"2065":{"id":"/docs/findings/reproduced-20","page_id":"/docs/findings/reproduced","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/findings/reproduced#in-the-sdks","content":"| Where                   | What                                                                                          | Upstream |\n| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n| `go-stellar-sdk` v0.9.0 | `strkey.DecodeAny` accepts SEP-0023's invalid claimable-balance key with a non-zero type byte | , , open |","table":"table-0"},"2066":{"id":"/docs/hazards","page_id":"/docs/hazards","type":"page","content":"The hazard registry","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards"},"2067":{"id":"/docs/hazards-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"Every named way readers of the Stellar ledger break, in eight layers, each with its evidence. The corpus grows by covering them."},"2068":{"id":"/docs/hazards-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards#how-to-read-a-hazard","content":"How to read a hazard"},"2069":{"id":"/docs/hazards-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"**What it is.** Every known way a program reading the Stellar ledger goes wrong, in  layers, each with its evidence."},"2070":{"id":"/docs/hazards-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"**Why it matters.** It is the corpus's blueprint: each entry targets hazards from this list, and each hazard says which check exercises it."},"2071":{"id":"/docs/hazards-4","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"`caps/registry.json` is the blueprint for the corpus. Each **hazard** is a named way a reader of the Stellar ledger breaks. Some examples:"},"2072":{"id":"/docs/hazards-5","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"a muxed account stored without its ID;"},"2073":{"id":"/docs/hazards-6","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"a contract call that creates a trustline with no event;"},"2074":{"id":"/docs/hazards-7","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"a failed transaction that still changes state;"},"2075":{"id":"/docs/hazards-8","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"a valid ledger a decoder refuses."},"2076":{"id":"/docs/hazards-9","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards","content":"Every hazard carries its **evidence**: an upstream issue, a protocol rule or a measurement. Every corpus entry names the hazards it targets."},"2077":{"id":"/docs/hazards-10","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards#how-to-read-a-hazard","content":"| Field | What it says                              |\n| ----- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| `id`  | `H-` + layer letter + number, e.g. `H-A3` |","table":"table-0"},"2078":{"id":"/docs/hazards-11","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards#how-to-read-a-hazard","content":"| Field   | What it says             |\n| ------- | ------------------------ |\n| `title` | The failure, in one line |","table":"table-0"},"2079":{"id":"/docs/hazards-12","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards#how-to-read-a-hazard","content":"| Field      | What it says                                                           |\n| ---------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `evidence` | Where it was seen: issues, pull requests, protocol rules, measurements |","table":"table-0"},"2080":{"id":"/docs/hazards-13","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards#how-to-read-a-hazard","content":"| Field  | What it says                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| ------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `test` | What the corpus holds or will hold for it. `C5` and `S11` are classic and Soroban recipe ledgers; `c-base` and `s-base` name a recipe step; `M:` names a shape in the mined mainnet ledgers (`mined/index.json`); `todo` marks a placement not yet built |","table":"table-0"},"2081":{"id":"/docs/hazards-14","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards#how-to-read-a-hazard","content":"| Field    | What it says                                                                                                |\n| -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `checks` | Which check will exercise it: replay probe, conformance, linter, guide, verify facts (the guide is planned) |","table":"table-0"},"2082":{"id":"/docs/hazards-15","page_id":"/docs/hazards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards#how-to-read-a-hazard","content":"The **covered** column lists the hazards an entry already targets. The rest are the corpus's to-do list, in the open."},"2083":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","type":"page","content":"Layer A: Addresses and identities","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-a"},"2084":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","content":"Every hazard in layer A of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2085":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","content":"Muxed accounts, signed-payload, hash-x and pre-auth signers, contract, liquidity-pool and claimable-balance addresses, and fee-bump sources: the places where an address is longer, or stranger, than a G key."},"2086":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","content":"**Why it matters.** An address is the key an indexer files everything under. When it is longer or stranger than a G… key, rows land under the wrong account, or the insert fails."},"2087":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-a","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2088":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","type":"page","content":"Layer B: Values and encodings","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-b"},"2089":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","content":"Every hazard in layer B of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2090":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","content":"Asset ordering, map keys that collide, amounts past 2⁵³, wide integers, invalid UTF-8, maximum lengths, nesting depth, and an authorization signed over bytes it does not contain: values that change, or break, when they are converted, decoded or verified."},"2091":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","content":"**Why it matters.** A value that changes when it is converted is a wrong number in front of a user: an amount rounded, a map key lost, a signature that won't verify."},"2092":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-b","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2093":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","type":"page","content":"Layer C: Transaction lifecycle and results","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-c"},"2094":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","content":"Every hazard in layer C of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2095":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","content":"Failed transactions that still change state, fee bumps, sponsorship, Soroban failures, order-book matching, liquidity pools, claimable balances, and fees that differ from what was bid."},"2096":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","content":"**Why it matters.** Whether a transaction succeeded, what it cost and what it changed are three different questions, and the ledger answers each in a different place."},"2097":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-c","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2098":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","type":"page","content":"Layer D: State changes no operation explains","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-d"},"2099":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","content":"Every hazard in layer D of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2100":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","content":"Ledger entries created, updated or removed by something other than an operation that names them: trustlines from contract calls, eviction, restores, revocation."},"2101":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","content":"**Why it matters.** A reader that only looks at operations misses state that changed for other reasons, and its balances drift away from the ledger."},"2102":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-d","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2103":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","type":"page","content":"Layer E: Events","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-e"},"2104":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","content":"Every hazard in layer E of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2105":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","content":"Unified events (CAP-0067), event data carrying muxed IDs and deep maps, and diagnostic events emitted twice."},"2106":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","content":"**Why it matters.** Events are the easiest way to follow contract activity, and the easiest to over-trust: some repeat, some carry deep data, some come from failed calls."},"2107":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-e","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2108":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","type":"page","content":"Layer F: Ledger level","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-f"},"2109":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","content":"Every hazard in layer F of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2110":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","content":"Upgrade ledgers, empty and very large ledgers, parallel Soroban stages, change order that varies between core processes, and the same ledger in different bytes."},"2111":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","content":"**Why it matters.** Code tested on ordinary ledgers meets the unusual ones at upgrades and peaks: empty, huge, reordered or upgraded ledgers."},"2112":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-f","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2113":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","type":"page","content":"Layer G: Protocol and network variants","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-g"},"2114":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","content":"Every hazard in layer G of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2115":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","content":"Bytes that depend on the network passphrase, older protocols, mainnet-only code paths in core, and CAPs gated before mainnet."},"2116":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","content":"**Why it matters.** The same transaction can be different bytes on another network or protocol. Code that assumes one of them breaks on the other."},"2117":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-g","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2118":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","type":"page","content":"Layer H: Reader robustness (SDK-only)","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Hazards","Layers"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/hazards/layer-h"},"2119":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h-0","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","content":"Every hazard in layer H of the registry, each with its evidence and the test the corpus holds or will hold for it."},"2120":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h-1","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","content":"Depth bombs, over-length vectors, truncated input and non-canonical encodings that an SDK must reject safely."},"2121":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h-2","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","content":"**Why it matters.** An SDK is the first thing to touch untrusted bytes. It must refuse bad input cleanly; crashing, or quietly accepting it, passes the problem on."},"2122":{"id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h-3","page_id":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/hazards/layer-h","content":"The hazards come straight from `caps/registry.json`. A tag says which check will exercise each one: **replay probe**, **conformance**, **linter**, **guide** or **verify facts**. **In the corpus** means an entry already targets it."},"2123":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"page","content":"Architecture","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Start"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture"},"2124":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-0","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture","content":"How the parts fit: where the ledgers come from, where 'correct' comes from, the three tools that run the corpus against real code, and what the Stellar Community Fund grant would fund."},"2125":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-1","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"Built today, and what the grant funds"},"2126":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-2","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"The RFP's deliverables, mapped"},"2127":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-3","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-the-ledgers-come-from","content":"Where the ledgers come from"},"2128":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-4","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-correct-comes-from","content":"Where \"correct\" comes from"},"2129":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-5","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#three-tools-that-run-it-against-real-code","content":"Three tools that run it against real code"},"2130":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-6","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#replay-for-indexers","content":"Replay, for indexers"},"2131":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-7","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#conformance-for-sdks","content":"Conformance, for SDKs"},"2132":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-8","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-linter-for-indexer-source","content":"The linter, for indexer source"},"2133":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-9","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#findings-and-disclosure","content":"Findings and disclosure"},"2134":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-10","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/architecture#distribution-and-ci","content":"Distribution and CI"},"2135":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-11","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture",