-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                                                                 | State | Re-run it            |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- | -------------------- |\n| Hazard registry:  named ways readers of the ledger break, in  layers | built | `caps/registry.json` |","table":"table-0"},"2143":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-19","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                                                                                       | State | Re-run it                      |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----- | ------------------------------ |\n| Corpus:  entries,  variants, from a pinned stellar-core v30.0.0 and SDF's public data lake | built | `make verify`, `make generate` |","table":"table-0"},"2144":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-20","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                                                                                                                                                     | State | Re-run it     |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- | ------------- |\n| Independent readers: stellar-core's own decoder and a schema walker find  expected values in their ledgers; `make verify` checks each at its exact field | built | `make oracle` |","table":"table-0"},"2145":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-21","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                                                                                          | State | Re-run it              |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- | ---------------------- |\n| Coverage:  of  union arms reached; the other  each classified with a stellar-core source line | built | `make coverage-report` |","table":"table-0"},"2146":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-22","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                                                                                                         | State | Re-run it          |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----- | ------------------ |\n| SDK conformance runner:  SDK builds on the corpus and test vectors,  older releases on fixed-bug regressions | built | `make conformance` |","table":"table-0"},"2147":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-23","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                                                  | State | Re-run it     |\n| ----------------------------------------------------- | ----- | ------------- |\n| Indexer replay harness, with a wallet-backend adapter | built | `make replay` |","table":"table-0"},"2148":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-24","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                                                                 | State | Re-run it                         |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- | --------------------------------- |\n| `xplint`, a Go linter for the three ingestion mistakes the RFP names | built | `make lint-selftest`, `make lint` |","table":"table-0"},"2149":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-25","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"| Part                       | State    | Re-run it         |\n| -------------------------- | -------- | ----------------- |\n| Findings reported upstream | reported | Reported upstream |","table":"table-0"},"2150":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-26","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"Everything above is pre-award work. The $60,000 award funds 120 engineer-days through the v1.0 release:"},"2151":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-27","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"the indexer-hardening guide, reviewed by a stellar-core or platform engineer;"},"2152":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-28","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"per-CAP and golden-agreement reports, and a memory gate on replay;"},"2153":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-29","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"packages for JavaScript, Go and Rust, CI workflows and a GitHub Action;"},"2154":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-30","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"two more indexer adapters (stellar-etl and Horizon), and runners for five more SDKs (Java, Swift, Flutter/Dart, PHP and .NET);"},"2155":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-31","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"this documentation site."},"2156":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-32","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#built-today-and-what-the-grant-funds","content":"After v1.0, XDRProof is maintained for 18 months, outside the award budget."},"2157":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-33","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"| The RFP asks for                                                                 | XDRProof                                                                                                  |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| A corpus of real and synthetic XDR shapes, each tagged with its CAP and protocol | `entries/` and `manifest.json`; each variant records its protocol, its source and whether it is synthetic |","table":"table-1"},"2158":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-34","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"| The RFP asks for                       | XDRProof                                                                                                                              |\n| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Expected decoded values for each entry | each entry's facts, every one backed by construction, a snapshot, stellar-core, the walker or review; none rests on one decoder alone |","table":"table-1"},"2159":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-35","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"| The RFP asks for                      | XDRProof                                                      |\n| ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| A coverage report per CAP and per arm | `COVERAGE.md` per arm and per hazard today; per CAP is funded |","table":"table-1"},"2160":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-36","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"| The RFP asks for                           | XDRProof                                                                                                                                |\n| ------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Agreement with stellar-core's golden files | `make coverage-goldens` compares arm coverage today; a comparison of decoded field paths and values at a pinned core revision is funded |","table":"table-1"},"2161":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-37","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"| The RFP asks for                                   | XDRProof                                                                    |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| A replay harness for indexers, with bounded memory | `replay/` with a documented adapter contract; a memory gate in CI is funded |","table":"table-1"},"2162":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-38","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"| The RFP asks for                                                           | XDRProof                                                                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| A linter for the three ingestion mistakes, clean on current wallet-backend | `linter/` catches all three. On unmodified wallet-backend `main` it reports  findings, all true, and  with XDRProof's two tested patches, offered upstream. Until an unmodified current revision runs clean, this check stays open |","table":"table-1"},"2163":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-39","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"| The RFP asks for                                                    | XDRProof                                 |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| An indexer-hardening guide, reviewed by a core or platform engineer | funded, published only after that review |","table":"table-1"},"2164":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-40","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-rfps-deliverables-mapped","content":"| The RFP asks for        | XDRProof          |\n| ----------------------- | ----------------- |\n| Findings filed upstream | Reported upstream |","table":"table-1"},"2165":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-41","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-the-ledgers-come-from","content":"**Made on a bare stellar-core.** Recipes drive a standalone stellar-core v30.0.0, pinned by image digest, with no peers, test-only keys committed in the recipes and no real funds. `make generate` rebuilds every run twice from scratch and checks that each pair agrees.  entries come from here."},"2166":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-42","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-the-ledgers-come-from","content":"**Order is measured, not assumed.** stellar-core writes some changes in an order that varies between runs. `make order-scan` runs each recipe lane  times and records which ledgers vary; the corpus ships the rarer orderings as bytes, so a reader that depends on order fails."},"2167":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-43","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-the-ledgers-come-from","content":"**Mined from the real networks.** `mined/` keeps  mainnet and testnet ledgers from the public data lake, with their URLs and hashes, for shapes that are cheaper to find than to make.  entries come only from mined ledgers;  from both."},"2168":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-44","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-correct-comes-from","content":"Expected values never come from the SDK under test."},"2169":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-45","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-correct-comes-from","content":"`make verify` round-trips every variant, byte for byte, through native rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0 and checks every fact."},"2170":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-46","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-correct-comes-from","content":"`make oracle` looks for each checkable value with two readers that share no code with any SDK, stellar-core's own C++ decoder and XDRProof's schema walker, and fails if either does not find it."},"2171":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-47","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#where-correct-comes-from","content":"`make novelty` checks that no entry is redundant: over  atoms,  entries hold something no other entry does, and the other  differ by where the hazard sits."},"2172":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-48","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#replay-for-indexers","content":"Each corpus ledger is served to a real indexer build through a mock stellar-rpc, in a fresh session with its own database. The verdict comes from what the indexer stored, never from its logs. An indexer joins with a small `adapter.toml` and a few shell commands; wallet-backend is the reference. On `main`,  of  variants are handled and  halt. Replay"},"2173":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-49","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#conformance-for-sdks","content":"Each SDK build is wrapped in a small program that speaks one line-delimited JSON protocol, so a new language needs one small program. Every known difference is pinned with its cause, so a new failure and a fix upstream both fail the run until the expectation is updated. Conformance"},"2174":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-50","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#the-linter-for-indexer-source","content":"`xplint` is a set of Go analyzers, one per mistake the RFP names. It writes text, JSON and SARIF for GitHub code scanning, and it fires before each upstream fix of these mistakes and goes quiet after. Linter"},"2175":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-51","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#findings-and-disclosure","content":"Ordinary bugs are written up and reported upstream after a duplicate search, each with a figure, a reproduction and, where possible, a tested fix. Halts and crashes anyone can trigger are reported privately first, through SDF's security channel, and are not published until a fix ships or 90 days pass."},"2176":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-52","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#distribution-and-ci","content":"**npm:** `xdrproof`, the command-line tool, and `@xdrproof/corpus`, the corpus with a loader and test helpers for Vitest and Jest."},"2177":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-53","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#distribution-and-ci","content":"**Go** and **Rust:** the corpus as a Go module with a table-test helper, and a Rust loader crate."},"2178":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-54","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#distribution-and-ci","content":"**Workflows** with actions pinned by SHA and a read-only token by default. `check` already runs `make verify`, `make novelty` and the linter self-test on every push and pull request; the grant adds the oracle and coverage gate to it, then conformance, replay (with the memory gate), determinism, golden agreement, an upstream watch that opens a pull request when stellar-xdr, stellar-core or an SDK tags a release, and release with checksums and provenance."},"2179":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-55","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#distribution-and-ci","content":"**A GitHub Action**, `XDRProof/action`, that runs the checks on a pinned corpus in another team's CI and uploads SARIF."},"2180":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-56","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#protocol-upgrades","content":"stellar-xdr's definitions are the input. A new union arm shows up as unclassified coverage and fails `make coverage-report` until an entry or an exclusion covers it (a CI gate from Tranche 2); enum members and CAPs are counted but not yet gated. The upstream watch turns each release into a pull request with the coverage change. After v1.0, XDRProof is maintained for 18 months: new protocol shapes, issue triage, and a release for each protocol upgrade."},"2181":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-57","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#independence","content":"The corpus, schema, tools and adapter contract are Apache-2.0 and run from a clone, with no XDRProof service in the path, so anyone can keep using them whatever happens to the maintainer. GitHub hosts the source, CI and releases; this site is generated from the repository. The tools collect no analytics. Milestone status is posted publicly in `STATUS.md` and weekly in #scf-general on the Stellar developer Discord."},"2182":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-58","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#security","content":"The generator runs a standalone stellar-core (no peers) in Docker, with test-only keys committed in the recipes and no real funds or production credentials; its HTTP port is published on the host while it runs. The repository needs no secrets. Every generated ledger is reproducible from its recipe. The replay harness runs each indexer in a container with a memory limit; an indexer that is killed ends its session and is judged from its process and cursor. Halts and crashes anyone can trigger are reported privately first. The threat model, what is in place and what is still open, is on Security."},"2183":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-59","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#technology","content":"| Part    | Choice                                                               | Why                          |\n| ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| Ledgers | stellar-core v30.0.0 (Docker, pinned digest), SDF's public data lake | the bytes the network writes |","table":"table-2"},"2184":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-60","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#technology","content":"| Part               | Choice                                                  | Why                                 |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| Reference decoding | rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0 (native), stellar-core `dump-xdr` | independent of every SDK under test |","table":"table-2"},"2185":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-61","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#technology","content":"| Part    | Choice        | Why                                    |\n| ------- | ------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| Tooling | Node.js, Rust | the corpus tools and the native reader |","table":"table-2"},"2186":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-62","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#technology","content":"| Part   | Choice                   | Why                                                                   |\n| ------ | ------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Linter | Go, `golang.org/x/tools` | the language of wallet-backend, stellar-etl and the SDK's ingest code |","table":"table-2"},"2187":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-63","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#technology","content":"| Part   | Choice                                                  | Why                                  |\n| ------ | ------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |\n| Replay | Docker, TimescaleDB (wallet-backend's own pinned image) | each indexer in its real environment |","table":"table-2"},"2188":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-64","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#technology","content":"| Part    | Choice     | Why |\n| ------- | ---------- | --- |\n| Licence | Apache-2.0 |     |","table":"table-2"},"2189":{"id":"/docs/start/architecture-65","page_id":"/docs/start/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/architecture#technology","content":"The same document, with more detail, is `ARCHITECTURE.md` in the repository."},"2190":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","type":"page","content":"Concepts","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Start"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/concepts"},"2191":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-0","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"The handful of words the corpus uses: entry, variant, hazard, placement, fact, evidence, focus, change order."},"2192":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-1","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"**What it is.** The few words these pages use, each explained in one line."},"2193":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-2","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"**Why it matters.** The corpus is exact about what an entry, a variant or a fact is. Knowing these words makes every other page quicker to read."},"2194":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-3","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word      | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Entry** | One rare shape. A directory under `entries/` with an `entry.json` and one XDR file per variant, plus up to two alternate-ordering files when the change order varies. Its `id` is permanent and never reused. |","table":"table-0"},"2195":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-4","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word             | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Catalog file** | The hand-written source of an entry, `catalog/<id>.json`: its intent and facts, addressed relative to the focus transaction. `make build` (`tools/build.mjs`) compiles it into the entry. |","table":"table-0"},"2196":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-5","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word       | Meaning                                                                                                 |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Family** | The first part of an entry id, such as `failure`, `signer` or `archival`. The docs group entries by it. |","table":"table-0"},"2197":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-6","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word        | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Variant** | One ledger file of an entry, for one protocol, one network passphrase and one emitter-flag profile. Its key reads like `p30-mainnet-generated`; a `-galexie` or `-bare` suffix names a non-base profile. |","table":"table-0"},"2198":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-7","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word             | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Flag profile** | Which of stellar-core's six metadata flags a run sets: `base` (the shipped variants), `galexie` (all six, as public data lakes are written) or `bare` (none). The ledger is the same; the metadata is not. |","table":"table-0"},"2199":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-8","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word     | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                               |\n| -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Lane** | How a variant was made: `generated` by a standalone stellar-core, `mined` from ledger history, or `synthetic` (hand-built; none yet). The recipes are also split into two lanes, classic and Soroban. |","table":"table-0"},"2200":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-9","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word       | Meaning                                                                                                                                     |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Hazard** | A named way readers break, with its evidence, from `caps/registry.json`. For example, `H-A3` is a signed-payload signer stored as 33 bytes. |","table":"table-0"},"2201":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-10","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word          | Meaning                                                                               |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Placement** | Where in the XDR a hazard sits in this entry. For example, `SetOptionsOp.signer.key`. |","table":"table-0"},"2202":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-11","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word     | Meaning                                                                                                                                                 |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Fact** | A value a correct reader must find at a JSON Pointer path in the SEP-0051 rendering, as `equals`, `length` or `some` (a matching change, in any order). |","table":"table-0"},"2203":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-12","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word         | Meaning                                                                      |\n| ------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Evidence** | What backs a fact: `construction`, `snapshot`, `core`, `walker` or `review`. |","table":"table-0"},"2204":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-13","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word          | Meaning                                                                                         |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **SDK check** | A rule for an SDK helper rather than a value, such as \"return all 5 entries or raise an error\". |","table":"table-0"},"2205":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-14","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word      | Meaning                                                                                               |\n| --------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Focus** | The transaction (and operation) the entry is about, so a reader knows where to look in a full ledger. |","table":"table-0"},"2206":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-15","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word             | Meaning                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Change order** | Whether stellar-core always emits the operation's changes in the same order. When it doesn't, the entry pins an order-normalised hash and lists every ordering seen. |","table":"table-0"},"2207":{"id":"/docs/start/concepts-16","page_id":"/docs/start/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/concepts","content":"| Word        | Meaning                                                                                                                 |\n| ----------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Channel** | `stable`, `next` (gated CAPs before mainnet) or `embargoed` (never committed publicly). All entries today are `stable`. |","table":"table-0"},"2208":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","type":"page","content":"How it works","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Start"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/how-it-works"},"2209":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-0","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works","content":"Recipes drive a bare stellar-core to emit rare shapes; the bytes become entries with facts; independent readers check them, real indexers ingest them, and SDK builds read the same bytes."},"2210":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-1","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#1-make-the-shapes","content":"1. Make the shapes"},"2211":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-2","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"2. Keep the bytes, write down the truth"},"2212":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-3","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"3. Check the truth independently"},"2213":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-4","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#4-run-it-against-real-code","content":"4. Run it against real code"},"2214":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-5","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works","content":"**What it is.** The whole pipeline in four steps: make rare ledgers, write down what a correct reader must find, check that with independent readers, then run real indexers, SDKs and indexer code against it."},"2215":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-6","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works","content":"**Why it matters.** Each step is one `make` command, so any claim on this site can be re-checked."},"2216":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-7","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works","content":"The full map, with every tool and its numbers:"},"2217":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-8","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#1-make-the-shapes","content":"A **recipe** is a deterministic script that drives one fresh standalone stellar-core v30.0.0 over its HTTP port. The core image is pinned by digest. There is no RPC and no network (no peers); the keys are test-only, from fixed seeds, there are no real funds, and close times are fixed. Core writes every closed ledger as `LedgerCloseMeta`."},"2218":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-9","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#1-make-the-shapes","content":"Some shapes only happen on a real network, or are worth having as real network bytes, so they are **mined** from public ledger history instead. `mined/` holds  real mainnet and testnet ledgers;  entries are built from  of them, the testnet ledger where wallet-backend halted among them."},"2219":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-10","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"Each interesting ledger becomes a corpus **entry**, compiled from a hand-written catalog file. An entry holds:"},"2220":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-11","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"the raw bytes;"},"2221":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-12","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"the CAP and the hazard it targets;"},"2222":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-13","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"where in the XDR the hazard sits;"},"2223":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-14","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#2-keep-the-bytes-write-down-the-truth","content":"the **facts** a correct reader must find, each with what backs it."},"2224":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-15","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"| Check                                | Reader                                                                         | Target        |\n| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------- |\n| Byte-for-byte round trip, every fact | native rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0; the WASM build must agree wherever it can decode | `make verify` |","table":"table-0"},"2225":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-16","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"| Check                    | Reader                                                           | Target        |\n| ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------- |\n| Fact values also read by | stellar-core's own `dump-xdr` (C++) and XDRProof's schema walker | `make oracle` |","table":"table-0"},"2226":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-17","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"| Check                        | Reader                                         | Target          |\n| ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | --------------- |\n| Which union arms are reached | a Python walker over the v30.0 XDR definitions | `make coverage` |","table":"table-0"},"2227":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-18","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"| Check                                                                                  | Reader                             | Target                 |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- | ---------------------- |\n| Every arm reached by an entry, or classified with the core source line that decides it | the schema walker, per transaction | `make coverage-report` |","table":"table-0"},"2228":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-19","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#3-check-the-truth-independently","content":"| Check                 | Reader                                                                  | Target         |\n| --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------- |\n| No entry is redundant | leave-one-out over each entry's arms, hazards, sightings and placements | `make novelty` |","table":"table-0"},"2229":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-20","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#4-run-it-against-real-code","content":"| Against  | How                                                                                                                                                                             | Target             |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| The SDKs | builds of the JavaScript, WASM, Rust, Go and Python SDKs read every corpus variant, plus strkey, invalid-input and depth vectors;  older releases run the fixed-bug regressions | `make conformance` |","table":"table-1"},"2230":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-21","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#4-run-it-against-real-code","content":"| Against    | How                                                                                                          | Target        |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------- |\n| An indexer | real wallet-backend builds ingest every corpus ledger, one isolated session each, through a mock stellar-rpc | `make replay` |","table":"table-1"},"2231":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-22","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#4-run-it-against-real-code","content":"| Against             | How                                                                                                       | Target      |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------- |\n| Indexer source code | `xplint` follows each value from where it came from to where it is kept, for the three ingestion mistakes | `make lint` |","table":"table-1"},"2232":{"id":"/docs/start/how-it-works-23","page_id":"/docs/start/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/how-it-works#4-run-it-against-real-code","content":"The figure marks two pieces as planned: a GitHub Action and the hardening guide. Today every check runs as a `make` target. Architecture says what is built and what the grant funds."},"2233":{"id":"/docs/start","page_id":"/docs/start","type":"page","content":"What XDRProof is","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Start"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/start"},"2234":{"id":"/docs/start-0","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"A shared corpus of rare but valid Stellar ledger data, the facts a correct reader must find in it, and tools that replay it into indexers and test SDKs on the same shapes."},"2235":{"id":"/docs/start-1","page_id":"/docs/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start#one-ledger-start-to-finish","content":"One ledger, start to finish"},"2236":{"id":"/docs/start-2","page_id":"/docs/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"What is in it"},"2237":{"id":"/docs/start-3","page_id":"/docs/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start#check-every-claim-yourself","content":"Check every claim yourself"},"2238":{"id":"/docs/start-4","page_id":"/docs/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start#what-it-has-found","content":"What it has found"},"2239":{"id":"/docs/start-5","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"**What it is.** A free, open test kit for any program that reads the Stellar ledger: indexers, wallets, explorers and SDKs. It holds real ledger data in its rarest valid shapes, the answers a correct program must give, and tools that check your code against them."},"2240":{"id":"/docs/start-6","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"**Why it matters.** Most ledgers are ordinary, so the rare shapes go untested until one arrives in production, where it can stop an indexer or store a wrong balance. XDRProof lets every team meet them in a test first."},"2241":{"id":"/docs/start-7","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"An indexer has run fine for months. Then a ledger arrives with a shape its code has never seen:"},"2242":{"id":"/docs/start-8","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"a signer that carries a payload;"},"2243":{"id":"/docs/start-9","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"a trustline created by a contract call, with no event;"},"2244":{"id":"/docs/start-10","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"a map whose keys all read as \"1\"."},"2245":{"id":"/docs/start-11","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"Ingestion halts, or worse, it keeps going and stores something wrong. The team fixes it. Next month another team, in another language, meets the same shape."},"2246":{"id":"/docs/start-12","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start","content":"**XDRProof turns each of those shapes into a test everyone can run.** One team's incident becomes every team's regression test, and one SDK's fix becomes a check on all of them."},"2247":{"id":"/docs/start-13","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part       | What it is                                                                                                     | Where      |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| **Corpus** | entries of real `LedgerCloseMeta` bytes, each tagged with its CAP, with the facts a correct reader must return | `entries/` |","table":"table-0"},"2248":{"id":"/docs/start-14","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part                | What it is                                                   | Where                |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------- |\n| **Hazard registry** | named ways readers break, in  layers, each with its evidence | `caps/registry.json` |","table":"table-0"},"2249":{"id":"/docs/start-15","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part                    | What it is                                                           | Where       |\n| ----------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------- |\n| **Schema and manifest** | JSON Schema 2020-12 for an entry; a generated index of every variant | `schema/v1` |","table":"table-0"},"2250":{"id":"/docs/start-16","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part              | What it is                                                                                                                                 | Where    |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------- |\n| **Mined ledgers** | real mainnet and testnet ledgers from the public data lake, for shapes only a real network produces, or worth having as real network bytes | `mined/` |","table":"table-0"},"2251":{"id":"/docs/start-17","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part        | What it is                                                                              | Where      |\n| ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| **Recipes** | Transactions for a bare standalone stellar-core that regenerate every generated variant | `recipes/` |","table":"table-0"},"2252":{"id":"/docs/start-18","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part      | What it is                                                                                   | Where    |\n| --------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n| **Tools** | Verify, oracle, coverage, generate, order scan, conformance and replay, each a `make` target | `tools/` |","table":"table-0"},"2253":{"id":"/docs/start-19","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-is-in-it","content":"| Part       | What it is                                                                                                                                                           | Where     |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------- |\n| **Linter** | `xplint`, which points at the lines of Go indexer code that make the three ingestion mistakes:  real findings on wallet-backend `main`,  with XDRProof's two patches | `linter/` |","table":"table-0"},"2254":{"id":"/docs/start-20","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#check-every-claim-yourself","content":"The numbers on these pages come from the repository's own data or from a `make` target you can run, and each page names which. Start with Quickstart."},"2255":{"id":"/docs/start-21","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-it-has-found","content":"Its runs found two bugs nobody had reported. Both are filed upstream with a figure and a reproduction: wallet-backend#834 and js-stellar-xdr-json#67. For an issue that was already open, wallet-backend#820, XDRProof offered a tested fix. See Reported upstream."},"2256":{"id":"/docs/start-22","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-it-has-found","content":"The RFP also asks for an **indexer-hardening guide**, and XDRProof will ship as a **GitHub Action** and packages for JavaScript, Go and Rust. These are what the grant funds; see Architecture. The registry already marks the hazards the guide will cover."},"2257":{"id":"/docs/start-23","page_id":"/docs/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start#what-it-has-found","content":"This repository backs a proposal to the Stellar Community Fund's RFP *XDR/CAP-Coverage Audit Tool and Indexer-Hardening Guide* (SCF #46). The corpus grows entry by entry from here. XDRProof is an independent open-source project, not affiliated with or endorsed by the Stellar Development Foundation."},"2258":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","type":"page","content":"Quickstart","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Start"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/quickstart"},"2259":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-0","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart","content":"Clone the repository and run make verify: strkey vectors, every entry against its schema, hashes, a byte-for-byte round trip and every fact, and every mined ledger, in seconds."},"2260":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-1","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/quickstart#get-the-code","content":"Get the code"},"2261":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-2","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"Check what you have"},"2262":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-3","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"Verify the corpus"},"2263":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-4","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/quickstart#go-further","content":"Go further"},"2264":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-5","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart","content":"**What it is.** The shortest path from nothing to a checked corpus: clone the repository, run one command, read the result."},"2265":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-6","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart","content":"**Why it matters.** You don't have to trust any number on this site. `make verify` re-checks every entry on your own machine in seconds."},"2266":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-7","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target        | Needs                         |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `make verify` | Node ≥ 22.15 and Rust (cargo) |","table":"table-0"},"2267":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-8","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target          | Needs                                                                         |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `make coverage` | Python 3 and Node ≥ 22.15 (`coverage-goldens` also needs git and the network) |","table":"table-0"},"2268":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-9","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target                                               | Needs                              |\n| ---------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `make coverage-report`, `make novelty`, `make build` | Node ≥ 22.15, npm and Rust (cargo) |","table":"table-0"},"2269":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-10","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target        | Needs                     |\n| ------------- | ------------------------- |\n| `make oracle` | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, npm |","table":"table-0"},"2270":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-11","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target                                                    | Needs                                     |\n| --------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| `make generate`, `make generate-quick`, `make order-scan` | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, npm, curl, jq, perl |","table":"table-0"},"2271":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-12","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target             | Needs              |\n| ------------------ | ------------------ |\n| `make conformance` | Node, Rust, Docker |","table":"table-0"},"2272":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-13","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target        | Needs                                                              |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `make replay` | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, Rust, git; the network on the first run only |","table":"table-0"},"2273":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-14","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#check-what-you-have","content":"| Target                            | Needs                                                             |\n| --------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `make lint-selftest`, `make lint` | Docker (a pinned Go image); `make lint` also Node ≥ 22.15 and git |","table":"table-0"},"2274":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-15","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"The first run installs the tool dependencies (`npm ci`) and builds the native reader (`cargo build --release`), about a minute. Then it checks:"},"2275":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-16","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"every SEP-0023 strkey test vector;"},"2276":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-17","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"every entry against the schema, and its hazards against the registry;"},"2277":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-18","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"size and sha256;"},"2278":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-19","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"a decode and re-encode through **native rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0** that must reproduce identical bytes;"},"2279":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-20","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"that the focus transaction is in the ledger;"},"2280":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-21","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"every expected fact;"},"2281":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-22","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"that the WASM build `@stellar/stellar-xdr-json`, wherever it can decode, renders the same JSON;"},"2282":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-23","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"that `manifest.json` is current;"},"2283":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-24","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#verify-the-corpus","content":"every mined ledger and its findings."},"2284":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-25","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#go-further","content":"Each target has its own page under Tools. `make all` runs verify, oracle, coverage, generate, conformance, replay, lint-selftest and lint."},"2285":{"id":"/docs/start/quickstart-26","page_id":"/docs/start/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/quickstart#go-further","content":"The steps above run XDRProof's own checks. To run the corpus in **your** test suite, lint **your** Go indexer, or replay it into **your** indexer, start at Use it on your code: a copy-paste test for JavaScript, Go, Rust and Python, and the one-line linter command."},"2286":{"id":"/docs/start/security","page_id":"/docs/start/security","type":"page","content":"Security","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Start"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/security"},"2287":{"id":"/docs/start/security-0","page_id":"/docs/start/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/security","content":"How to report a vulnerability in XDRProof, how XDRProof handles the bugs it finds in other projects, and its threat model: what it runs, what it protects, the threats with what is in place and what is planned, and the risks still open."},"2288":{"id":"/docs/start/security-1","page_id":"/docs/start/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/security#report-a-vulnerability-in-xdrproof","content":"Report a vulnerability in XDRProof"},"2289":{"id":"/docs/start/security-2","page_id":"/docs/start/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/security#bugs-xdrproof-finds-in-other-projects","content":"Bugs XDRProof finds in other projects"},"2290":{"id":"/docs/start/security-3","page_id":"/docs/start/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/security#the-threat-model","content":"The threat model"},"2291":{"id":"/docs/start/security-4","page_id":"/docs/start/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/security#what-it-protects","content":"What it protects"},"2292":{"id":"/docs/start/security-5","page_id":"/docs/start/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/security#where-untrusted-things-come-in","content":"Where untrusted things come in"},"2293":{"id":"/docs/start/security-6","page_id":"/docs/start/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/security#threats-and-what-stands-against-each","content":"Threats, and what stands against each"},"2294":{"id":"/docs/start/security-7","page_id":"/docs/start/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/security#still-open","content":"Still open"},"2295":{"id":"/docs/start/security-8","page_id":"/docs/start/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/start/security#status","content":"Status"},"2296":{"id":"/docs/start/security-9","page_id":"/docs/start/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/security","content":"**What it is.** How to report a security problem in XDRProof, how XDRProof handles the bugs it finds in other people's code, and its threat model: what it protects, and how."},"2297":{"id":"/docs/start/security-10","page_id":"/docs/start/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/security","content":"**Why it matters.** Teams will use the corpus's expected values as the right answers, and run XDRProof's tools on their own machines and in their CI. Both only work if they can be trusted, so this page says what is in place today, what is planned, and what is still open."},"2298":{"id":"/docs/start/security-11","page_id":"/docs/start/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/security#report-a-vulnerability-in-xdrproof","content":"Report it privately through GitHub: **Security → Report a vulnerability** on the repository. Please don't open a public issue. XDRProof has one maintainer, so these are targets, not guarantees: a reply within 3 working days, and a fix or a plan within 30 days. You are credited in the release notes unless you would rather not be named."},"2299":{"id":"/docs/start/security-12","page_id":"/docs/start/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/security#report-a-vulnerability-in-xdrproof","content":"In scope is anything in the repository that could mislead a user or harm their machine or CI: a corpus entry or expected value that is wrong; a tool, testee or adapter that runs or leaks more than it should; a workflow that exposes a secret or can be hijacked; and, once they ship, the release artifacts and packages. The policy itself is `SECURITY.md`."},"2300":{"id":"/docs/start/security-13","page_id":"/docs/start/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/security#bugs-xdrproof-finds-in-other-projects","content":"Anything that lets someone halt or crash an indexer, an SDK or stellar-core is reported **privately first**, through the owner's security channel, and is not published until a fix ships or 90 days pass. Behaviour a project documents as by design is recorded in the findings report, not filed. Everything else is filed publicly, with a figure, a reproduction and, where possible, a tested fix: see Reported upstream. The rules are in `findings/policy.md`."},"2301":{"id":"/docs/start/security-14","page_id":"/docs/start/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/security#the-threat-model","content":"What follows is read from `THREAT-MODEL.md`, a draft of . Tranche 2 completes it in SDF's STRIDE template, with a data-flow diagram, a numbered threat list, verification evidence and a monitoring plan: what is watched, what triggers action, who responds and how fast."},"2302":{"id":"/docs/start/security-15","page_id":"/docs/start/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/security#the-threat-model","content":"Nothing in XDRProof runs as a hosted service. It runs on your machine or in CI: the generator is a standalone stellar-core in Docker with test-only keys and no real funds, the conformance runner executes published SDK packages, the replay harness builds and runs a real indexer in Docker, and the linter analyses other projects' Go source in a pinned container."},"2303":{"id":"/docs/start/security-16","page_id":"/docs/start/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/start/security#status","content":"`STATUS.md` is a dated public log of what has landed: reports filed, sibling sweeps, CI, and wording corrected to say exactly what was measured. Weekly updates also go to #scf-general on the Stellar developer Discord."},"2304":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"page","content":"Conformance","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance"},"2305":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance","content":"make conformance: every corpus variant, the strkey vectors, invalid XDR and a depth ladder through builds of the JavaScript, Go, Rust and Python SDKs. Each build's known failures are written down first; anything else fails the run."},"2306":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"The builds"},"2307":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"What each family checks"},"2308":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-recorded-matrix","content":"The recorded matrix"},"2309":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance#every-known-failure-and-why","content":"Every known failure, and why"},"2310":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/conformance#how-deep-each-sdk-reads","content":"How deep each SDK reads"},"2311":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance","content":"**What it is.** The same corpus bytes and test vectors, run through  builds of the JavaScript, WebAssembly, Rust, Go and Python SDKs, with every answer compared;  older releases run the fixed-bug regressions."},"2312":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance","content":"**Why it matters.** When two SDKs read the same bytes differently, one of them is wrong, and every app built on it inherits the mistake. This finds it first."},"2313":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance","content":"Needs Node ≥ 22.15, Rust and Docker. Every SDK build answers the same questions about the same bytes, and the run prints one matrix: which SDK reads which ledger correctly."},"2314":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"Each build runs as a **testee**: a small process that reads one JSON request per line on stdin and answers one JSON line on stdout. The runner (`conformance/run.mjs`) judges every answer against a reference and against that build's pre-registered expectations."},"2315":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee | Package                                                                                     | Families |\n| ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n|        | `@stellar/stellar-sdk` 17.4.0 (its XDR layer is `@stellar/js-xdr` 5, written in JavaScript) | all      |","table":"table-0"},"2316":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee | Package                                                             | Families              |\n| ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------- |\n|        | `@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0, rs-stellar-xdr compiled to WASM | corpus, lane S, depth |","table":"table-0"},"2317":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee | Package                                          | Families                      |\n| ------ | ------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------- |\n|        | `stellar-xdr` 30.0.0 with `stellar-strkey` 1.0.0 | all but the native projection |","table":"table-0"},"2318":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee | Package                                    | Families                                        |\n| ------ | ------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------- |\n|        | `github.com/stellar/go-stellar-sdk` v0.9.0 | all but SEP-0051 JSON and the native projection |","table":"table-0"},"2319":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-14","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee | Package                                                     | Families |\n| ------ | ----------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |\n|        | `stellar-sdk` 17.0.1 (py-stellar-base), the current release | all      |","table":"table-0"},"2320":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-15","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee | Package                                                                                                | Families                      |\n| ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------- |\n|        | `stellar-xdr` 0.2.1 and `stellar-strkey` 0.2.0, which py-stellar-base's next major release is built on | corpus, strkey, lane S, depth |","table":"table-0"},"2321":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-16","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"| Testee                                                              | Package                                      | Families  |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | --------- |\n| `js@17.2.0` · `js@17.2.1` · `js@17.3.0` · `go@v0.7.1` · `go@v0.7.2` | the releases before and after upstream fixes | sightings |","table":"table-0"},"2322":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-17","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-builds","content":"Java, Swift, Dart, PHP and .NET SDKs can join by implementing the same protocol: `hello`, `decode`, `encode_json`, `strkey_decode` and `native`. A testee answers `unsupported` for anything its SDK cannot do, and that is recorded as not applicable, not as a failure."},"2323":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-18","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family   | Input                        | Passes when                                                                                                                                                           |\n| -------- | ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `corpus` | every variant of every entry | it decodes; the re-encoding is byte-identical; where the SDK renders SEP-0051 JSON, the JSON equals rs-stellar-xdr's and every expected fact of the entry holds on it |","table":"table-1"},"2324":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-19","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family   | Input                                                                                         | Passes when                                                                |\n| -------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `strkey` | the SEP-0023 test vectors, plus a deterministic sample of every kind of address in the corpus | valid keys decode to the right kind and payload; invalid keys are rejected |","table":"table-1"},"2325":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-20","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family   | Input                                                                                                                                                                                                                       | Passes when                                                      |\n| -------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `lane-s` | hand-built invalid XDR: truncated, trailing bytes, a bool of 2, unknown and negative discriminants, a symbol past its maximum, non-zero padding, an optional flag of 2, a huge length with no data, a path longer than five | every invalid input is rejected; the valid controls are accepted |","table":"table-1"},"2326":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-21","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family  | Input                                     | Passes when                                                                |\n| ------- | ----------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `depth` | an `ScVal` vector nested 64 to 4,000 deep | measured: the deepest value each SDK decodes, and whether anything crashes |","table":"table-1"},"2327":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-22","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family      | Input                              | Passes when                                                                                           |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `sdk-check` | each entry's `expected.sdk_checks` | the helper behaves as the check says, for example a native projection keeps every map entry or raises |","table":"table-1"},"2328":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-23","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#what-each-family-checks","content":"| Family      | Input                                | Passes when                                                            |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `sightings` | `conformance/vectors/sightings.json` | the upstream fix behaves as fixed; the releases before it show the bug |","table":"table-1"},"2329":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-24","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-recorded-matrix","content":"This is `conformance/recorded/matrix.md` from the last run. **known** means a failure that is written down in that build's expectations, with its cause."},"2330":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-25","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#the-recorded-matrix","content":"`js@17.4.0` and `python-xdr@0.2.1` read every corpus variant exactly as rs-stellar-xdr does: the bytes, the SEP-0051 JSON and every expected fact."},"2331":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-26","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#every-known-failure-and-why","content":"Each build's `conformance/expectations/<testee>.json` lists every check it is known to fail, with the reason and the finding it belongs to. They are written from a measured run and reviewed by hand. After that the run must match them exactly: **an unexpected failure and an unexpected pass both fail it**, so a new bug and an upstream fix both show up."},"2332":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-27","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#how-deep-each-sdk-reads","content":"Nothing crashes on the depth ladder; each SDK refuses cleanly past its limit. The deepest sampled depth each one decodes:"},"2333":{"id":"/docs/tools/conformance-28","page_id":"/docs/tools/conformance","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/conformance#how-deep-each-sdk-reads","content":"A depth limit counts XDR nesting, and one `ScVal` level costs several units, so equal limits don't mean equal reach: the WASM decoder and the Python packages both use 500 and differ fourfold. The corpus has ledgers stellar-core applied whose Soroban authorization tree is 245 levels deep, and the WASM decoder refuses them (finding F-1 on Findings)."},"2334":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","type":"page","content":"Coverage","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/coverage"},"2335":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"make coverage and make coverage-report: every XDR union arm reachable from LedgerCloseMeta is either reached by a corpus entry or classified with the stellar-core source line that decides it."},"2336":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/coverage#every-arm-accounted-for","content":"Every arm accounted for"},"2337":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"**What it is.** A count of every kind of data a ledger can hold (every branch of every XDR union), and whether the corpus has a real example of each."},"2338":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"**Why it matters.** It turns \"we test the rare cases\" into a number. Every branch the corpus lacks has a written reason, with the stellar-core source line behind it."},"2339":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"A walker reads the rs-stellar-xdr definitions for v30.0, and lists every union arm and enum member reachable from `LedgerCloseMeta`. It then walks real ledgers and records which arms they meet."},"2340":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"| Ledgers                          | Union arms               |\n| -------------------------------- | ------------------------ |\n| Reachable from `LedgerCloseMeta` | **** (and  enum members) |","table":"table-0"},"2341":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"| Ledgers                                                                                           | Union arms |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| Entry files ( variant files: raw `.xdr`, alternate change orderings, and `.xdr.zst` decompressed) | of         |","table":"table-0"},"2342":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"| Ledgers                    | Union arms |\n| -------------------------- | ---------- |\n| The mined ledgers ( files) | of         |","table":"table-0"},"2343":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"| Ledgers                            | Union arms |\n| ---------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| Entries and mined ledgers together | of         |","table":"table-0"},"2344":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"| Ledgers                                                               | Union arms |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| stellar-core's goldens (866 files, 15,306 ledgers, commit `f2b91947`) | 254 of 499 |","table":"table-0"},"2345":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage","content":"The corpus rows are computed from the corpus at commit , the same way `make coverage` computes them. The goldens row is a fixed measurement at stellar-core commit `f2b91947`. The goldens are the ledgers stellar-core's own tests produce."},"2346":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage#every-arm-accounted-for","content":"`make coverage` counts every arm in an entry's file, including arms from other transactions that happen to share its ledger. `make coverage-report` is stricter. An entry **reaches** an arm only when its own transaction (envelope, fee changes, apply meta) or its ledger's own shape (header, transaction-set phases, upgrades, evicted keys) contains it. Every arm no entry reaches is classified in `coverage/exclusions.json`, with the stellar-core source line that decides it. The run fails if an arm is in neither list, or in both."},"2347":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage#every-arm-accounted-for","content":"Enum members reached by an entry:  of . The report is written to `COVERAGE.md`, with the entries that target each hazard."},"2348":{"id":"/docs/tools/coverage-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/coverage","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/coverage#every-arm-accounted-for","content":"No `todo` arm is left: every arm stellar-core can still write at protocol 30 on a standalone network is in an entry. Only the  `network-only` arms, which need a live network, are out of reach. A closed protocol-30 ledger never carries a `validation-only` or `not-emitted` arm, and a `historical` one appears only in ledgers of older protocols."},"2349":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"page","content":"Generate","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate"},"2350":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate","content":"make generate: 20 fresh standalone stellar-core v30.0.0 runs, in pairs, over both lanes, both passphrases, P30 and P28 and three flag profiles. Every pair must agree, and every shipped generated variant must come out again."},"2351":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"The matrix"},"2352":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate#what-must-hold","content":"What must hold"},"2353":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate#each-pair-agrees","content":"Each pair agrees"},"2354":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate#every-shipped-variant-is-reproduced","content":"Every shipped variant is reproduced"},"2355":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-profiles-are-compared","content":"The profiles are compared"},"2356":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate","content":"**What it is.** Rebuilds every generated ledger from scratch on fresh, offline stellar-cores, twice, and checks that the runs agree."},"2357":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate","content":"**Why it matters.** It proves the corpus is reproducible: nothing in it depends on our machine, our timing or our luck."},"2358":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate","content":"It needs Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, npm, curl, jq and perl, and host ports 21700–21719. The stellar-core image is pinned by digest in `recipes/core-image`, and every run refuses anything but stellar-core v30.0.0."},"2359":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"`make generate` runs **10 pairs**, which is 20 fresh standalone cores, 6 at a time. There is no RPC and no network: each core is standalone (no peers), with test-only keys committed in the recipes and no real funds."},"2360":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"| Lane    | Protocol | Passphrase       | Flag profile |\n| ------- | -------- | ---------------- | ------------ |\n| Classic | P30      | mainnet, testnet | base         |","table":"table-0"},"2361":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"| Lane    | Protocol | Passphrase | Flag profile  |\n| ------- | -------- | ---------- | ------------- |\n| Classic | P30      | mainnet    | galexie, bare |","table":"table-0"},"2362":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"| Lane    | Protocol | Passphrase       | Flag profile |\n| ------- | -------- | ---------------- | ------------ |\n| Soroban | P30      | mainnet, testnet | base         |","table":"table-0"},"2363":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"| Lane    | Protocol | Passphrase       | Flag profile |\n| ------- | -------- | ---------------- | ------------ |\n| Soroban | P28      | mainnet, testnet | base         |","table":"table-0"},"2364":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-14","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-matrix","content":"| Lane    | Protocol | Passphrase | Flag profile  |\n| ------- | -------- | ---------- | ------------- |\n| Soroban | P30      | mainnet    | galexie, bare |","table":"table-0"},"2365":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-15","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#each-pair-agrees","content":"The two runs of a pair are compared ledger by ledger. They must be byte-identical, or identical after normalising. Normalising sorts the order of changes within an operation and zeroes the diagnostic `invoke_time_nsecs` timing value."},"2366":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-16","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#every-shipped-variant-is-reproduced","content":"The list of shipped generated variants is read from `entries/` (`tools/shipped-checks.mjs`), so the testnet and protocol-28 variants are re-checked too. A full run also fails if any shipped variant was not assigned to a pair."},"2367":{"id":"/docs/tools/generate-17","page_id":"/docs/tools/generate","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/generate#the-profiles-are-compared","content":"The same recipe ledgers under the three flag profiles are compared, and the result is recorded in `recipes/<lane>/profiles/p30-mainnet.json`. Every ledger keeps the same header hash; only the metadata changes. See Found while building."},"2368":{"id":"/docs/tools","page_id":"/docs/tools","type":"page","content":"Tools","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools"},"2369":{"id":"/docs/tools-0","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"Every check is a make target: verify, oracle, coverage, novelty, generate, order scan, conformance and replay. Each one re-runs a claim and prints its result."},"2370":{"id":"/docs/tools-1","page_id":"/docs/tools","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"The readers behind them"},"2371":{"id":"/docs/tools-2","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"**What it is.** Every check XDRProof runs, each one a `make` target."},"2372":{"id":"/docs/tools-3","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"**Why it matters.** Every number on this site comes from one of these commands, and each prints its result on your machine."},"2373":{"id":"/docs/tools-4","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target        | What it proves                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               | Needs              | Time    |\n| ------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ------- |\n| `make verify` | Strkey vectors hold. Every entry matches the schema, sizes and hashes match, and every variant round-trips byte for byte through native rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0. The focus transaction is present, every fact holds, the WASM reader renders the same JSON wherever it can decode, and the manifest is current. Every mined ledger is verified | Node ≥ 22.15, Rust | seconds |","table":"table-0"},"2374":{"id":"/docs/tools-5","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target        | What it proves                                                                          | Needs                     | Time    |\n| ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- | ------- |\n| `make oracle` | Each fact's values are also read by stellar-core's own decoder and by the schema walker | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, npm | seconds |","table":"table-0"},"2375":{"id":"/docs/tools-6","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target          | What it proves                                                                                  | Needs                  | Time    |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ------- |\n| `make coverage` | union arms are reachable from `LedgerCloseMeta` at v30.0; the entries reach , the mined ledgers | Python 3, Node ≥ 22.15 | seconds |","table":"table-0"},"2376":{"id":"/docs/tools-7","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target                 | What it proves                                                                                                                                                              | Needs              | Time    |\n| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ------- |\n| `make coverage-report` | Every one of the  arms is reached by an entry's own transaction or ledger shape () or classified with the stellar-core source line that decides it (). Writes `COVERAGE.md` | Node ≥ 22.15, Rust | seconds |","table":"table-0"},"2377":{"id":"/docs/tools-8","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target         | What it proves                                                                                        | Needs              | Time    |\n| -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ------- |\n| `make novelty` | No entry is redundant: leave-one-out over  atoms (arms, enum members, hazards, sightings, placements) | Node ≥ 22.15, Rust | seconds |","table":"table-0"},"2378":{"id":"/docs/tools-9","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target                  | What it proves                                                  | Needs                  | Time        |\n| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ----------- |\n| `make coverage-goldens` | stellar-core's own 866 golden ledger files reach 254 of the 499 | Python 3, git, network | about 1 min |","table":"table-0"},"2379":{"id":"/docs/tools-10","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target          | What it proves                                                                                                                                                                      | Needs                             | Time         |\n| --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- | ------------ |\n| `make generate` | 20 fresh standalone cores, in pairs, across both lanes, both passphrases, P30 and P28 and three flag profiles. Every pair agrees, and every shipped generated variant is reproduced | Docker, Node, npm, curl, jq, perl | about 10 min |","table":"table-0"},"2380":{"id":"/docs/tools-11","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target                | What it proves                      | Needs    | Time        |\n| --------------------- | ----------------------------------- | -------- | ----------- |\n| `make generate-quick` | The two mainnet base P30 pairs only | the same | about 3 min |","table":"table-0"},"2381":{"id":"/docs/tools-12","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target            | What it proves                                                              | Needs  | Time         |\n| ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------ | ------------ |\n| `make order-scan` | fresh runs per lane: which ledgers vary in change order; content never does | Docker | about 15 min |","table":"table-0"},"2382":{"id":"/docs/tools-13","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target             | What it proves                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          | Needs              | Time             |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | ---------------- |\n| `make conformance` | Every corpus variant, the strkeys, the invalid-input vectors and a depth ladder go through  SDK builds;  older releases run the fixed-bug regressions. js-stellar-sdk 17.4.0 and `stellar-xdr` 0.2.1 read every variant exactly as rs-stellar-xdr does; every other difference is pinned with its cause | Node, Rust, Docker | about 2 min warm |","table":"table-0"},"2383":{"id":"/docs/tools-14","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target        | What it proves                                                                                                                                 | Needs                           | Time              |\n| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| `make replay` | The whole corpus into wallet-backend `main` ( of  handled,  halted), and every fixed failure before and after its fix. Every verdict is pinned | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, Rust, git | about 20 min cold |","table":"table-0"},"2384":{"id":"/docs/tools-15","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target               | What it proves                                                                            | Needs  | Time             |\n| -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------ | ---------------- |\n| `make lint-selftest` | The linter catches all  seeded violations and all  mutants, and flags none of  good cases | Docker | about 2 min warm |","table":"table-0"},"2385":{"id":"/docs/tools-16","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"| Target      | What it proves                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Needs                     | Time             |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- | ---------------- |\n| `make lint` | The linter on real code: wallet-backend `main` ( findings, all true;  with the two patches), stellar-etl (), go-stellar-sdk ingest (), the commits around upstream fixes and fixed bugs put back. Every finding is pinned and triaged | Docker, Node ≥ 22.15, git | about 3 min warm |","table":"table-0"},"2386":{"id":"/docs/tools-17","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"`make all` runs verify, oracle, coverage, generate, conformance, replay, lint-selftest and lint."},"2387":{"id":"/docs/tools-18","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools","content":"`make build` is not a check: it compiles `catalog/` into `entries/` from the runs of `make generate` and `mined/`, in minutes. See Catalog."},"2388":{"id":"/docs/tools-19","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader        | What it is                                                                                                         | Used by                                                               |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Native reader | A small Rust program on `stellar-xdr` 30.0.0 (depth limit 1500) that decodes, renders SEP-0051 JSON and re-encodes | verify, novelty, coverage-report, conformance (the reference), replay |","table":"table-1"},"2389":{"id":"/docs/tools-20","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader      | What it is                                           | Used by             |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| WASM reader | `@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0, a second opinion | verify, conformance |","table":"table-1"},"2390":{"id":"/docs/tools-21","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader         | What it is                                                                | Used by |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |\n| `stellar-core` | Core's own C++ `dump-xdr`, in Docker with no network, on the pinned image | oracle  |","table":"table-1"},"2391":{"id":"/docs/tools-22","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader        | What it is                                                                                                         | Used by |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------- |\n| Schema walker | `walker.mjs`: XDRProof's own JavaScript reader, driven only by the XDR definitions; it shares no code with any SDK | oracle  |","table":"table-1"},"2392":{"id":"/docs/tools-23","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader          | What it is                                                                                                         | Used by  |\n| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------- |\n| Coverage walker | `xdrcov.py`: a Python walker over the same v30.0 definitions that records every union arm and enum member it meets | coverage |","table":"table-1"},"2393":{"id":"/docs/tools-24","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader | What it is                                                                       | Used by             |\n| ------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| xplint | Go analyzers over SSA (`golang.org/x/tools`), built and run in a pinned Go image | lint, lint-selftest |","table":"table-1"},"2394":{"id":"/docs/tools-25","page_id":"/docs/tools","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools#the-readers-behind-them","content":"| Reader         | What it is                                                                                                                             | Used by                  |\n| -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------ |\n| Segment walker | the schema walker again, attributing every arm it meets to the transaction envelope or result meta it sits in, or to the ledger itself | coverage-report, novelty |","table":"table-1"},"2395":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"page","content":"Linter","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter"},"2396":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter","content":"make lint and make lint-selftest: xplint reads Go indexer code and points at the lines that make the three ingestion mistakes the RFP names. Text, JSON or SARIF for GitHub code scanning. Every finding on real code is triaged and pinned."},"2397":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#run-it","content":"Run it"},"2398":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#the-three-mistakes","content":"The three mistakes"},"2399":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#how-it-decides","content":"How it decides"},"2400":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#on-real-code","content":"On real code"},"2401":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#wallet-backend-main","content":"wallet-backend `main`"},"2402":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#stellar-etl-master","content":"stellar-etl `master`"},"2403":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#before-and-after-each-upstream-fix","content":"Before and after each upstream fix"},"2404":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#fixed-bugs-put-back","content":"Fixed bugs put back"},"2405":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#self-test","content":"Self-test"},"2406":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/linter#limits","content":"Limits"},"2407":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter","content":"**What it is.** `xplint` reads the Go source of an indexer and points at the lines that make one of three mistakes: trusting a field stellar-core never checked, walking a nested structure with no limit, or taking a transaction's participants from what it asked for instead of what happened."},"2408":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter","content":"**Why it matters.** These three mistakes are how real indexers have halted or stored wrong data. The linter finds the code patterns themselves, whether or not a ledger has triggered them yet. On wallet-backend `main` it reports , all real code patterns and none reachable by a ledger today: one is hardening at today's limits, three sit in code Blend's contracts cannot reach. With XDRProof's two patches it reports ."},"2409":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#run-it","content":"The exit status is 0 with no findings, 1 with findings, and 2 when the packages could not be loaded. It needs the same Go toolchain and cgo setup the packages build with."},"2410":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-14","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#run-it","content":"In this repository, both targets run in a pinned Go image (`linter/toolchain-image`) through `linter/go.sh`:"},"2411":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-15","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#the-three-mistakes","content":"rules,  sub-rules on by default ( in all). Each sub-rule has a stable ID, used as the SARIF `ruleId`. The table is read from `linter/xplint/rules.go`."},"2412":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-16","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#how-it-decides","content":"**Where a value came from.** Each value carries labels for its source: text bytes, a possibly muxed address, a number a contract chose, the declared footprint, the authorization tree, an operation body, a diagnostic event. Sources are named SDK calls, conversions and field reads."},"2413":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-17","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#how-it-decides","content":"**Where it is kept.** A sink is where a value is stored: a JSON- or db-tagged field, a map key, a set member, a builder's account. A map or set counts only when it outlives the function; a scratch map for de-duplication is not attribution."},"2414":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-18","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#how-it-decides","content":"**What clears it.** A check counts only when every path to the sink passes through it, and only for the value it checked: checking `good` does not clear `bad + good`. A success check clears operation bodies and events on its branch."},"2415":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-19","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#how-it-decides","content":"**Recursion.** XP002 finds recursive walks over the XDR types that can nest, computed from the SDK's own type graph, so CAP-0071's nested delegates are covered without a list. A budget must stop the walk; a budget that only logs, or that fails the ledger, is reported."},"2416":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-20","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#on-real-code","content":"Not yet on unmodified `main`: its  findings are real. With XDRProof's two tested patches, offered upstream, xplint reports . Until an unmodified current revision runs clean, this check stays open."},"2417":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-21","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#wallet-backend-main","content":"The unbounded walk is wallet-backend#820: today's deepest tree (245 levels) survives it, so it is hardening. XDRProof's patch bounds it at depth 256 and 4,096 nodes and stops, counted and logged, instead of failing; it is offered on #820. The three Blend sites are the shape wallet-backend fixed in its SEP-41 processor (#756), not reachable today. Both patches are in `linter/patches/`; with them, wallet-backend's own build, vet and tests pass."},"2418":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-22","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#before-and-after-each-upstream-fix","content":"The same rules on the wallet-backend commits around fixes of these mistakes. Each fixed bug is caught before its fix and gone after it."},"2419":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-23","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#fixed-bugs-put-back","content":"Mutants of current wallet-backend `main`: a fixed bug put back, or a guard removed. Each must make its rule fire."},"2420":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-24","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#self-test","content":"`make lint-selftest` runs xplint on seeded fixtures, each modelled on real code and named in its fixture, then mutates the good cases."},"2421":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-25","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#self-test","content":"The near misses are seeded too: a check on a different memo than the one stored, a bound on the wrong branch, `max` used as a bound, a helper that calls the UTF-8 check and ignores it, a budget that only logs, a method that happens to be called `Successful`."},"2422":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-26","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#limits","content":"Calls through interfaces and function values are not followed (closures are), nor values sent over channels."},"2423":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-27","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#limits","content":"Flows through a database are not followed: wallet-backend #802 (Blend decimals stored, read back and used as an exponent) is out of reach; the in-memory shape is caught."},"2424":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-28","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#limits","content":"Memory is tracked one struct field deep; deeper fields and slice elements share their root's labels."},"2425":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-29","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#limits","content":"The sources and sinks are a catalogue of go-stellar-sdk and standard-library calls (`xplint/sdk.go`); code that reads XDR through its own types needs entries added."},"2426":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-30","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#limits","content":"No false-positive rate is claimed. Every finding on the three codebases and on the history commits is triaged true, but three codebases are too few to state a rate."},"2427":{"id":"/docs/tools/linter-31","page_id":"/docs/tools/linter","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/linter#limits","content":"The full list is in `linter/README.md`."},"2428":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","type":"page","content":"Novelty","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/novelty"},"2429":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"make novelty: leave-one-out over every entry's arms, enum members, hazards, sightings and placements. The run fails if any entry holds nothing another entry doesn't."},"2430":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"Today"},"2431":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"**What it is.** A check that every entry earns its place: each must hold something no other entry holds."},"2432":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"**Why it matters.** The corpus grows by purpose, not by volume, so a team can run all of it without wading through duplicates."},"2433":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"The corpus grows by purpose, not volume. Novelty checks that every entry earns its place. It describes each entry as a set of **atoms**:"},"2434":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"| Atom                        | What counts                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| --------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Union arms and enum members | those reached by the entry's own transaction (its envelope and result meta) or by its ledger's own shape (header, transaction-set phases, upgrades, evicted keys), in any variant. Other transactions that share the ledger count for nothing |","table":"table-0"},"2435":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"| Atom    | What counts                   |\n| ------- | ----------------------------- |\n| Hazards | each hazard the entry targets |","table":"table-0"},"2436":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"| Atom      | What counts                       |\n| --------- | --------------------------------- |\n| Sightings | each upstream issue it reproduces |","table":"table-0"},"2437":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"| Atom       | What counts                                        |\n| ---------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| Placements | each pair of a hazard and where in the XDR it sits |","table":"table-0"},"2438":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty","content":"An entry is **redundant** when every one of its atoms is also held by some other entry. Each entry is left out in turn, so the result does not depend on the order entries are read, unlike a greedy \"does it add anything new\" pass. The run exits 1 if any entry is redundant."},"2439":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"At commit :  redundant entries, over  atoms."},"2440":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"| Entries                                                           | Count |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- |\n| Hold an arm, enum member, hazard or sighting no other entry holds |       |","table":"table-1"},"2441":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"| Entries                        | Count |\n| ------------------------------ | ----- |\n| Told apart only by a placement |       |","table":"table-1"},"2442":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"| Entries                                                        | Count |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------- | ----- |\n| … of which every other atom sits inside one single other entry |       |","table":"table-1"},"2443":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-14","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"<Callout title=\"Placement is a claim, not a measurement\">A placement is text the author wrote. So `make novelty` lists every entry told apart only by placement, and flags the ones wholly contained in one other entry: for each such pair, the facts must check a different thing.</Callout>"},"2444":{"id":"/docs/tools/novelty-15","page_id":"/docs/tools/novelty","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/novelty#today","content":"It needs Node ≥ 22.15 and the native reader (Rust), which finds each variant's transaction hashes."},"2445":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","type":"page","content":"Oracle","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/oracle"},"2446":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"make oracle: each fact's values must also appear in what stellar-core's own C++ decoder reads and in what XDRProof's schema walker reads. No SDK is involved."},"2447":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"A planted error must be caught"},"2448":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"**What it is.** A second and a third opinion on every expected value, from two readers that share no code with any SDK: stellar-core's own decoder and XDRProof's own walker."},"2449":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"**Why it matters.** A test is only as good as its expected answer. Readers that cannot share a bug with the SDK under test keep the answers honest."},"2450":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"A fact is only as good as the reader that produced it. The oracle asks two readers that share no code with any SDK:"},"2451":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"| Reader           | How it runs                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **stellar-core** | `stellar-core dump-xdr`, inside `docker run --network none` on the `stellar/quickstart` image pinned by digest in `recipes/core-image` (override with `XDRPROOF_CORE_IMAGE`) |","table":"table-0"},"2452":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"| Reader            | How it runs                                                                                                                                 |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Schema walker** | `tools/walker.mjs`, driven only by the v30.0 XDR definitions. It rejects non-zero padding, over-length values, bad enums and trailing bytes |","table":"table-0"},"2453":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle","content":"For each fact, the oracle takes its expected values and checks that they appear among the values each reader produced. Enum values are checked too: `tools/coverage/defs/sep0051-enum-names.json` maps every XDR enum member to the string SEP-0051 prints for it, so the walker confirms a member it reached and stellar-core one its dump printed. Enum names are matched by name across all enums, anywhere in the ledger, so a common name such as `success` is a weak confirmation. A `length` fact has no value to check, and a `v0`-style arm name is structure, not a value. A value either reader misses is a **disagreement**, and the run fails. So does a ledger stellar-core cannot print: nothing in it is confirmed. With `--write`, it records `core` and `walker` in the fact's evidence."},"2454":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"A reader that finds every value might simply accept anything. So the oracle tests itself. It takes every expected value of 10 or more digits (64-bit amounts, IDs and fees), changes its last digit and asks both readers again."},"2455":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"| Result                                | Counts as |\n| ------------------------------------- | --------- |\n| Both readers reject the changed value | caught    |","table":"table-1"},"2456":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"| Result                                                                      | Counts as                    |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| Both readers find it: the changed value occurs elsewhere in the same ledger | a coincidence, not a failure |","table":"table-1"},"2457":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"| Result                   | Counts as                         |\n| ------------------------ | --------------------------------- |\n| Only one reader finds it | a **disagreement**: the run fails |","table":"table-1"},"2458":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"If no planted error is caught at all, the self-test fails and the run exits 1."},"2459":{"id":"/docs/tools/oracle-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/oracle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/oracle#a-planted-error-must-be-caught","content":"<Callout title=\"What it does not check\">It checks that a value is present in a reader's output, not that it sits at the fact's path; the path is checked by Verify.</Callout>"},"2460":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","type":"page","content":"Order scan","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/order-scan"},"2461":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan","content":"make order-scan: 20 fresh runs per recipe lane record which ledgers come out in more than one byte order, and prove the content never varies."},"2462":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"The committed results"},"2463":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan","content":"**What it is.** A measurement of how often stellar-core writes the same ledger's changes in a different order."},"2464":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan","content":"**Why it matters.** Code that assumes a fixed order works on one run and breaks on the next. The corpus ships the rarer orderings, so that code fails in a test instead."},"2465":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan","content":"stellar-core iterates a hash map with a per-process random salt, so the changes within an operation can come out in a different order on every run. The order scan measures this. It runs each recipe lane 20 times on fresh cores, records every byte ordering seen per ledger, and fails if any ledger's **content** differs once order is normalised."},"2466":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"Results are in `recipes/classic/order-scan/p30-mainnet-base.json` and `recipes/soroban/order-scan/p30-mainnet-base.json`:"},"2467":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"| Lane    | Runs | Ledgers that vary in order               | Content that varies |\n| ------- | ---- | ---------------------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| Classic |      | of  recipe ledgers;  differ in every run | 0                   |","table":"table-0"},"2468":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"| Lane    | Runs | Ledgers that vary in order | Content that varies |\n| ------- | ---- | -------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| Soroban |      | of  recipe ledgers         | 0                   |","table":"table-0"},"2469":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"The protocol-upgrade ledger (2) varies in both lanes as well; it is never an entry."},"2470":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"Corpus entries whose order varies pin an order-normalised hash, and list every raw ordering seen (`change_order` in the schema)."},"2471":{"id":"/docs/tools/order-scan-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/order-scan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/order-scan#the-committed-results","content":"<Callout type=\"warn\" title=\"For indexers\">Never depend on the order of changes within an operation.</Callout>"},"2472":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"page","content":"Replay","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay"},"2473":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay","content":"make replay: every corpus ledger into a real indexer through a mock stellar-rpc, one fresh store per ledger. Each variant gets a verdict from the indexer's own state: its cursor, its process and what it stored. Never from logs."},"2474":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#wallet-backend-main-the-whole-corpus","content":"wallet-backend main, the whole corpus"},"2475":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#every-fix-before-and-after","content":"Every fix, before and after"},"2476":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#a-session","content":"A session"},"2477":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#derive-the-probes","content":"Derive the probes"},"2478":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#serve-the-ledger","content":"Serve the ledger"},"2479":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#prepare-start-and-watch","content":"Prepare, start and watch"},"2480":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#observe","content":"Observe"},"2481":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"Verdicts"},"2482":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"Probes"},"2483":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/replay#bring-your-own-indexer","content":"Bring your own indexer"},"2484":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay","content":"**What it is.** Every corpus ledger fed to a real indexer, one at a time, through a mock of the RPC an indexer reads ledgers from, then a look at what the indexer stored."},"2485":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay","content":"**Why it matters.** An indexer that halts stops a wallet's history; one that stores a wrong balance is worse. Replay shows which ledgers do that before mainnet does."},"2486":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay","content":"Needs Docker, Node ≥ 22.15 and the native reader (`make native`). Once the images and Go modules are cached nothing reaches the network: the ledgers come from the corpus. On a laptop under Docker, six sessions at a time, the full run takes about 9.5 minutes once the builds are cached."},"2487":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-14","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#wallet-backend-main-the-whole-corpus","content":"The reference indexer is stellar/wallet-backend. At `main` () it ingests  variants in  sessions: ** handled,  halted**. The other  are each pinned with their cause in `replay/expectations/wallet-backend@main.json`:"},"2488":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-15","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#every-fix-before-and-after","content":"Each fixed failure the corpus reproduces is replayed at the commits before its fix and at the fix (`replay/sightings.json`). Each must fail before and pass at the fix."},"2489":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-16","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#a-session","content":"One session replays one ledger, one distinct variant (its bytes and its network), into a fresh store."},"2490":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-17","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#derive-the-probes","content":"`replay/lib/derive.mjs` reads the ledger with rs-stellar-xdr and lists the **probes**: the facts a correct indexer has stored after ingesting it. They come from the ledger alone."},"2491":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-18","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#serve-the-ledger","content":"`replay/lib/mock-rpc.mjs` serves the ledger as stellar-rpc v30.0.1 would: `getLedgers`, `getHealth`, `getLatestLedger`, `getNetwork`, `getVersionInfo`, and `getLedgerEntries` from the state the ledger leaves behind. Pagination, page limits and validation errors follow stellar-rpc word for word."},"2492":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-19","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#serve-the-ledger","content":"The mock knows only what the ledger states, so it knows less than stellar-rpc in three ways, each recorded per session: a method it does not serve, or a request such as a history-archive fetch, is refused; a ledger entry the ledger does not touch is answered \"not found\", though on a real network it may exist; and a contract entry whose TTL the ledger leaves untouched is served without `liveUntilLedgerSeq`. A verdict other than `handled` in a session that read such state is flagged and traced in the indexer's code before it is recorded."},"2493":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-20","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#prepare-start-and-watch","content":"The adapter gives the indexer a clean store whose cursor sits just before the ledger and starts it against the mock. The harness polls the cursor until it passes the ledger, the process exits, the cursor stops moving, or the attempt times out. Once the ledger is passed, the process must still be running a second later. If the ledger was not passed, the indexer is started once more on the same store."},"2494":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-21","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#observe","content":"The adapter answers every probe from what the indexer stored, and the harness compares."},"2495":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-22","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#observe","content":"Before any session counts, the harness replays the **controls**: the plainest ledger of each protocol the target can decode. Each must get past its ledger, or the run stops as a setup failure."},"2496":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-23","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict   | Rule                                                            |\n| --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `handled` | the cursor passed the ledger and every applicable probe matches |","table":"table-0"},"2497":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-24","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict   | Rule                                                          |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `dropped` | the cursor passed, and a fact the ledger states is not stored |","table":"table-0"},"2498":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-25","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict      | Rule                                                                                 |\n| ------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `mishandled` | the cursor passed, and a stored value differs from the ledger, or an invariant fails |","table":"table-0"},"2499":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-26","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict   | Rule                                                                                             |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `stalled` | the first attempt stopped making progress on the ledger without exiting; the restart got past it |","table":"table-0"},"2500":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-27","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict   | Rule                                                            |\n| --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `crashed` | the first attempt exited on the ledger; the restart got past it |","table":"table-0"},"2501":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-28","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict  | Rule                              |\n| -------- | --------------------------------- |\n| `halted` | neither attempt passed the ledger |","table":"table-0"},"2502":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-29","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict       | Rule                                                                                    |\n| ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `unsupported` | the ledger's protocol is newer than the target decodes, or its network is not supported |","table":"table-0"},"2503":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-30","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#verdicts","content":"| Verdict | Rule                                                         |\n| ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `error` | an adapter command failed; never a finding (the run exits 2) |","table":"table-0"},"2504":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-31","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"Each probe names a fact by its key and states the expected value in a canonical form: strkeys, decimal strings, XDR enum names. A muxed `M…` account is named by its `G…` account."},"2505":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-32","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                                                                         | Expected                                                                                                          |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `tx.present`, `tx.result`, `tx.fee_charged`, `tx.operations`, `tx.inner_hash` | the transaction is stored, with its result code, fee charged, operation count and, for a fee bump, the inner hash |","table":"table-1"},"2506":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-33","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                              | Expected                                                                                              |\n| ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `tx.participant`, `op.participant` | the source and fee source are attributed to the transaction; each operation's source to its operation |","table":"table-1"},"2507":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-34","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                                                    | Expected                                                                            |\n| -------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `account.created`, `account.removed`, `contract.created` | the accounts and contract instances the transaction created or removed are recorded |","table":"table-1"},"2508":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-35","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                                              | Expected                                           |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `signer.added`, `signer.updated`, `signer.removed` | the signer change is recorded, with its new weight |","table":"table-1"},"2509":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-36","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                                  | Expected                                                     |\n| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `trustline.added`, `trustline.removed` | the trustline the transaction created or removed is recorded |","table":"table-1"},"2510":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-37","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"| Probe                                 | Expected                                                          |\n| ------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `balance.native`, `balance.trustline` | the balance at the end of the ledger, where the ledger changes it |","table":"table-1"},"2511":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-38","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#probes","content":"Two **invariants** cover everything the indexer stored in the session: `addresses.valid` (every stored address decodes and is one the ledger names) and `facts.extra` (every stored creation, merge and trustline change is one the ledger states). Facts on which indexers differ on purpose are not asserted."},"2512":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-39","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#bring-your-own-indexer","content":"The indexer is reached only through an **adapter**: a directory under `replay/adapters/` with an `adapter.toml` and a few shell commands (`prepare`, `start` and `cursor` are required; `observe`, `setup`, `stop`, `finish` and `teardown` are optional). Any indexer that reads `getLedgers` can be tested the same way. An adapter that implements only the three required commands still gets `handled`, `crashed` and `halted` for every ledger. The full contract is in replay/README.md."},"2513":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-40","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#bring-your-own-indexer","content":"The wallet-backend adapter builds each commit in a pinned Go image, runs TimescaleDB 2.28.2 as its compose file does, copies each session's database from a migrated template, runs `wallet-backend ingest` in live mode against the mock, and answers probes with one read of the session database."},"2514":{"id":"/docs/tools/replay-41","page_id":"/docs/tools/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/replay#bring-your-own-indexer","content":"<Callout title=\"A new failure, a changed one and a fixed one all fail the run\">`replay/expectations/<adapter>@<target>.json` lists every variant that is not `handled`, with the exact probes that failed, the cause and the upstream issue. It also pins the set of variants judged (their count and a sha256 of their ids), so none can drop out of a run unnoticed.</Callout>"},"2515":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"page","content":"Verify","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Tools"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify"},"2516":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-0","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify","content":"make verify: strkey test vectors, then every entry against the schema, its hashes, a byte-for-byte round trip through native rs-stellar-xdr, the focus transaction and every fact; then every mined ledger."},"2517":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-1","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#what-it-checks","content":"What it checks"},"2518":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-2","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#strkeys","content":"Strkeys"},"2519":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-3","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-entry","content":"The entry"},"2520":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-4","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-bytes-and-the-round-trip","content":"The bytes and the round trip"},"2521":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-5","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-focus-and-the-facts","content":"The focus and the facts"},"2522":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-6","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#a-second-reader","content":"A second reader"},"2523":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-7","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-manifest","content":"The manifest"},"2524":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-8","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-mined-ledgers","content":"The mined ledgers"},"2525":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-9","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-create-contract-authorization-payload","content":"The create-contract authorization payload"},"2526":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-10","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tools/verify#output","content":"Output"},"2527":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-11","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify","content":"**What it is.** The basic check: every entry is exactly what it says it is, byte for byte, and every expected fact holds."},"2528":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-12","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify","content":"**Why it matters.** If the corpus were wrong, every test built on it would be wrong too. This runs in seconds, so it runs on every change."},"2529":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-13","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#strkeys","content":"Every SEP-0023 test vector must decode and re-encode (valid), or be rejected (invalid). This covers XDRProof's own strkey codec, which the schema walker uses."},"2530":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-14","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-entry","content":"The entry is validated against `schema/v1/entry.schema.json` (ajv, strict mode). Its `id` must match its directory, and every hazard id must exist in the registry. Variant keys must be unique, and each file name must start with its key."},"2531":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-15","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-bytes-and-the-round-trip","content":"Size and sha256 must match; `.zst` variants are checked on their decompressed bytes. The **native reader** (rs-stellar-xdr 30.0.0, depth limit 1500) decodes the bytes, renders SEP-0051 JSON and encodes them again. The result must be identical, byte for byte."},"2532":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-16","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-focus-and-the-facts","content":"The focus transaction hash must be in the ledger, as an outer or fee-bump inner hash. Every fact that applies to the variant must hold at its JSON Pointer path. If the entry's change order is unstable, the order-normalised hash must match, and the shipped bytes must be one of the orderings seen."},"2533":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-17","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#a-second-reader","content":"The WASM build `@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0 decodes each variant again, with no fallback. If it renders different JSON, the run fails. If it cannot decode a variant at all (for example at its depth limit), verify lists it and records `fails` in the manifest, and the run still passes. Today  of  variants agree."},"2534":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-18","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-manifest","content":"`manifest.json` is rebuilt from the entries. Without `--write`, verify fails if the committed file has drifted."},"2535":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-19","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-mined-ledgers","content":"`tools/mined-verify.mjs` checks every mined ledger. It must match its hash and size, round-trip through the native reader, and carry the recorded sequence and protocol. Every finding's transaction and JSON path must be present."},"2536":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-20","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#the-create-contract-authorization-payload","content":"For the two v1 create-contract entries, `tools/auth-payload-check.mjs` encodes each authorization preimage by hand twice, once as the entry's own bytes and once as the V2 rendering the host hashes, and verifies the ed25519 signature against both. The entry stellar-core accepted verifies only as V2; the one signed over its own bytes verifies only as v1, and core failed it. No SDK or host code is involved. See hazard H-B16."},"2537":{"id":"/docs/tools/verify-21","page_id":"/docs/tools/verify","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tools/verify#output","content":"A table with one row per variant: entry@variant, protocol, source, round trip, facts, WASM reader. Then a summary of the counts (entries, variants, hazards targeted), an `OK` line, and the mined-ledger report."},"2538":{"id":"/docs/use","page_id":"/docs/use","type":"page","content":"Use it on your code","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use it"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/use"},"2539":{"id":"/docs/use-0","page_id":"/docs/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use","content":"For SDK and indexer teams: test your own SDK against every corpus ledger, lint your Go indexer for the three ingestion mistakes, and replay the corpus into your indexer. Each page is a copy-paste start, run in a clean folder before it was written down."},"2540":{"id":"/docs/use-1","page_id":"/docs/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use","content":"**What it is.** The rest of these docs show how XDRProof tests other people's code. This section is for your team: the shortest way to point XDRProof at your own SDK, your own Go indexer and your own indexer."},"2541":{"id":"/docs/use-2","page_id":"/docs/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use","content":"**Why it matters.** The corpus holds the rare ledgers that broke real indexers and SDKs. Running it in your own CI means your code meets them before your users do, and keeps meeting them on every change."},"2542":{"id":"/docs/use-3","page_id":"/docs/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use","content":"<Card title=\"Test your SDK\" href=\"/docs/use/sdk\" description=\"A copy-paste test in JavaScript, Go, Rust or Python, with nothing to clone: every corpus ledger must…\" />"},"2543":{"id":"/docs/use-4","page_id":"/docs/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use","content":"<Card title=\"Lint your Go indexer\" href=\"/docs/use/lint\" description=\"Run xplint on your repository for the three ingestion mistakes, with SARIF for GitHub code scanning.\" />"},"2544":{"id":"/docs/use-5","page_id":"/docs/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use","content":"<Card title=\"Replay your indexer\" href=\"/docs/use/replay\" description=\"Write a small adapter and replay every corpus ledger into your indexer, with a verdict for each from…\" />"},"2545":{"id":"/docs/use-6","page_id":"/docs/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use","content":"Nothing to clone for the first two: the SDK test fetches the pinned corpus from GitHub and checks every file's sha256, and the linter runs straight from its Go module path. Replaying an indexer runs the harness from a clone today."},"2546":{"id":"/docs/use-7","page_id":"/docs/use","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use","content":"Every snippet in this section was run in an empty folder on 19 November 2026. The output shown is what it printed. Where something did not work as first written, the page says so."},"2547":{"id":"/docs/use/lint","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","type":"page","content":"Lint your Go indexer","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use it"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/use/lint"},"2548":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-0","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/lint","content":"Run xplint on your own Go repository with one command: it points at the lines that make the three ingestion mistakes, as text, JSON or SARIF for GitHub code scanning. Run from an empty folder against a fresh clone of wallet-backend."},"2549":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-1","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/use/lint#the-three-mistakes-in-plain-words","content":"The three mistakes, in plain words"},"2550":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-2","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/use/lint#run-it","content":"Run it"},"2551":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-3","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/use/lint#in-ci-sarif-for-github-code-scanning","content":"In CI: SARIF for GitHub code scanning"},"2552":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-4","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/lint","content":"**What it is.** `xplint` reads your Go indexer's source and points at the exact lines that make one of three mistakes. You run it on your own repository with one command; there is nothing to install first."},"2553":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-5","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/lint","content":"**Why it matters.** These three mistakes are how real indexers have halted or stored wrong data. Finding them in review costs a minute; finding them after a ledger arrives costs an incident."},"2554":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-6","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/lint#the-three-mistakes-in-plain-words","content":"| Rule      | The mistake                                                                                                                      | What to do instead                                                                                    |\n| --------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **XP001** | Trusting a field stellar-core does not check. Memo text, contract strings and muxed addresses are whatever the sender put there. | Validate the bytes (UTF-8, length), and switch on an address's kind before treating it as an account. |","table":"table-0"},"2555":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-7","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/lint#the-three-mistakes-in-plain-words","content":"| Rule      | The mistake                                                                                                              | What to do instead                                                                                                       |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| **XP002** | Walking a nested structure with no limit. Authorization trees and nested contract values can be hundreds of levels deep. | Carry a depth and a node budget, and at the limit stop descending. Never fail the ledger: it fails again on every retry. |","table":"table-0"},"2556":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-8","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/lint#the-three-mistakes-in-plain-words","content":"| Rule      | The mistake                                                                                                         | What to do instead                                                          |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **XP003** | Taking participants from the envelope. The envelope says what a transaction asked for; the meta says what happened. | Attribute accounts from the meta, and only for transactions that succeeded. |","table":"table-0"},"2557":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-9","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/lint#the-three-mistakes-in-plain-words","content":"Each rule has sub-rules with stable IDs; the full list, with the evidence behind each, is on Linter."},"2558":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-10","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/lint#run-it","content":"From your repository's root, with Go 1.26 or later and the same cgo setup your packages build with:"},"2559":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-11","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/lint#run-it","content":"Go fetches the linter's module straight from GitHub (`github.com/XDRProof/xdrproof/linter`); nothing is cloned or installed. It needs no module of its own either: run from an empty folder, `go run github.com/XDRProof/xdrproof/linter/cmd/xplint@latest -list` printed every rule. Run from an empty folder on a fresh clone of stellar/wallet-backend at `18db88f`, in the `golang:1.27` image with an empty module cache, it printed:"},"2560":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-12","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/lint#run-it","content":"{\"internal/indexer/processors/contract_deploy.go:116:2: XP002/walk-unbudgeted: closure walkInvocation recurses over SorobanAuthorizedInvocation with no depth or node budget\\ninternal/services/blend/processor.go:387:21: XP001/muxed-as-account: an address that may be muxed (M...) or not an account is passed to stageHistoryRow, where it reaches .WithAccount() at blend/processor.go:439; it comes from ScAddress.String at blend/scval.go:71\\ninternal/services/blend/processor.go:579:16: XP001/muxed-as-account: an address that may be muxed (M...) or not an account is passed to routeEntry, where it reaches a map key at blend/processor.go:819; it comes from ScAddress.String at blend/scval.go:71\\ninternal/services/blend/scval.go:182:6: XP001/muxed-as-account: an address that may be muxed (M...) or not an account reaches a map key; it comes from ScAddress.String at blend/scval.go:71\\nexit status 1\"}"},"2561":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-13","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/lint#run-it","content":"Each line is `file:line:col: RULE: message`. The exit status is 0 with no findings, 1 with findings, and 2 when the packages could not be loaded. All four of these are real; what each one means, and the patches that clear them, are on Linter."},"2562":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-14","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/lint#in-ci-sarif-for-github-code-scanning","content":"The same run wrote the same four findings as SARIF 2.1.0:"},"2563":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-15","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/lint#in-ci-sarif-for-github-code-scanning","content":"{\"      3 \\\"ruleId\\\": \\\"XP001/muxed-as-account\\\"\\n      1 \\\"ruleId\\\": \\\"XP002/walk-unbudgeted\\\"\"}"},"2564":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-16","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/lint#in-ci-sarif-for-github-code-scanning","content":"Upload `xplint.sarif` with GitHub's `github/codeql-action/upload-sarif` and each finding appears on its line in pull requests. Other flags: `-format json`, `-rules XP002,XP003/declared-auth` for a subset, and `-list` for every rule."},"2565":{"id":"/docs/use/lint-17","page_id":"/docs/use/lint","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/lint#in-ci-sarif-for-github-code-scanning","content":"xplint follows Go values through SSA within and across your packages. It does not follow calls through interfaces or function values, values sent over channels, or flows through a database, and it knows the go-stellar-sdk's accessors, not your own XDR types. A clean run means none of the patterns it knows, not no bugs. Limits"},"2566":{"id":"/docs/use/replay","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","type":"page","content":"Replay your indexer","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use it"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/use/replay"},"2567":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-0","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay","content":"Write a small adapter (a TOML file and a few shell commands) and the harness replays every corpus ledger into your indexer, one fresh session each, and judges each one from what your indexer stored. wallet-backend is the worked example."},"2568":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-1","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/use/replay#try-it-on-wallet-backend-first","content":"Try it on wallet-backend first"},"2569":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-2","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/use/replay#write-an-adapter-for-your-indexer","content":"Write an adapter for your indexer"},"2570":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-3","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/use/replay#what-a-verdict-means","content":"What a verdict means"},"2571":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-4","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay","content":"**What it is.** A way to feed every rare ledger in the corpus into your own indexer, the way an RPC would serve it, and get a verdict for each one: handled, dropped, mishandled, stalled, crashed or halted."},"2572":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-5","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay","content":"**Why it matters.** It is the closest thing to meeting those ledgers in production, without waiting for them. The verdict comes from your indexer's own state, so a wrong balance is caught even when nothing crashes."},"2573":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-6","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#try-it-on-wallet-backend-first","content":"The replay harness runs from the repository today, so this one needs a clone, with Docker and Node 22.15 or later. The planned `xdrproof` command-line tool (Tranche 1, by 26 January 2027) brings `xdrproof replay` without one."},"2574":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-7","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#try-it-on-wallet-backend-first","content":"A real run, with the Docker images already on this machine (a first run also downloads them and builds wallet-backend, a few minutes; each step says so on its live row):"},"2575":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-8","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#try-it-on-wallet-backend-first","content":"Before any session counts, one control ledger for each protocol the target decodes must get through; if one does not, the run stops, because the setup is wrong, not the indexer. When the indexer asks the mock for state the ledger does not hold (wallet-backend reads two Blend contracts at startup), the run says so under the cards, and a verdict that depends on such a read is traced by hand. Drop `--entries` to replay the whole corpus."},"2576":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-9","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#write-an-adapter-for-your-indexer","content":"An adapter is a folder under `replay/adapters/` with an `adapter.toml` and a few commands. Any indexer that reads ledgers through `getLedgers` can join. Below is a starting template, cut down from wallet-backend's adapter; fill in your own commands."},"2577":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-10","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#write-an-adapter-for-your-indexer","content":"| Command | Required | Does                                               |\n| ------- | -------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `setup` | no       | once per target: build the commit, start the store |","table":"table-0"},"2578":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-11","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#write-an-adapter-for-your-indexer","content":"| Command   | Required | Does                                                              |\n| --------- | -------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `prepare` | yes      | per session: a clean store with the cursor just before the ledger |","table":"table-0"},"2579":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-12","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#write-an-adapter-for-your-indexer","content":"| Command | Required | Does                                                                |\n| ------- | -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `start` | yes      | per session: run the indexer in the foreground against the mock RPC |","table":"table-0"},"2580":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-13","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#write-an-adapter-for-your-indexer","content":"| Command  | Required | Does                                        |\n| -------- | -------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n| `cursor` | yes      | print the last ledger the indexer committed |","table":"table-0"},"2581":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-14","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#write-an-adapter-for-your-indexer","content":"| Command   | Required | Does                                                                                                           |\n| --------- | -------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `observe` | no       | read `{\"probes\": [...]}` on stdin and answer each one `present` (with the stored value) or `absent`, read-only |","table":"table-0"},"2582":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-15","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#write-an-adapter-for-your-indexer","content":"| Command                      | Required | Does                                                 |\n| ---------------------------- | -------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| `stop`, `finish`, `teardown` | no       | stop the indexer, drop the session's store, clean up |","table":"table-0"},"2583":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-16","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#write-an-adapter-for-your-indexer","content":"Each command gets the session in its environment: `XDRPROOF_FIRST_LEDGER`, `XDRPROOF_LAST_LEDGER`, `XDRPROOF_NETWORK_PASSPHRASE`, `XDRPROOF_RPC_URL` (and a `_DOCKER` form that reaches the host from a container), `XDRPROOF_TARGET_REF` and more; the full list is in `replay/README.md`."},"2584":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-17","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#write-an-adapter-for-your-indexer","content":"With only `prepare`, `start` and `cursor`, every ledger already gets `handled`, `crashed` or `halted`. Add `observe` and the harness can also tell `dropped` and `mishandled`: it works out probes from the ledger's own meta (transactions, participants, signers, trustlines, balances, contracts) and asks your store about each one."},"2585":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-18","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#write-an-adapter-for-your-indexer","content":"The worked example is `replay/adapters/wallet-backend/`: it builds each commit in a pinned Go image, runs TimescaleDB as wallet-backend's compose file does, copies each session's database from a migrated template, and answers every probe with one read of the session database. Its `cursor.sh` is one SQL query."},"2586":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-19","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#what-a-verdict-means","content":"Each ledger gets two attempts: if the first does not get past it, the indexer is restarted once on the same store."},"2587":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-20","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#what-a-verdict-means","content":"| Verdict   | Means                                                                        |\n| --------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `handled` | the cursor passed the ledger and every probe your adapter answers matches it |","table":"table-1"},"2588":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-21","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#what-a-verdict-means","content":"| Verdict   | Means                                                            |\n| --------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `dropped` | the cursor passed, and something the ledger states is not stored |","table":"table-1"},"2589":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-22","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#what-a-verdict-means","content":"| Verdict      | Means                                                                                |\n| ------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `mishandled` | the cursor passed, and a stored value differs from the ledger, or an invariant fails |","table":"table-1"},"2590":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-23","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#what-a-verdict-means","content":"| Verdict   | Means                                                                                      |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `stalled` | the first attempt stopped making progress without exiting; the restart got past the ledger |","table":"table-1"},"2591":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-24","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#what-a-verdict-means","content":"| Verdict   | Means                                                           |\n| --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `crashed` | the first attempt exited on the ledger; the restart got past it |","table":"table-1"},"2592":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-25","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#what-a-verdict-means","content":"| Verdict  | Means                                                   |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `halted` | neither attempt got past the ledger: ingestion is stuck |","table":"table-1"},"2593":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-26","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#what-a-verdict-means","content":"Two more statuses are not findings: `unsupported` (the ledger's protocol is newer than your target decodes, or its network is not supported) and `error` (an adapter command failed; the run exits 2)."},"2594":{"id":"/docs/use/replay-27","page_id":"/docs/use/replay","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/replay#what-a-verdict-means","content":"See Replay for how sessions, probes and expectations work, and for wallet-backend's results across the whole corpus."},"2595":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","type":"page","content":"Test your SDK","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Use it"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/use/sdk"},"2596":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-0","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk","content":"A copy-paste test in JavaScript, Go, Rust or Python: every ledger in the XDRProof corpus must decode and re-encode to the exact same bytes. Nothing to clone: the test fetches the pinned corpus and checks every file's sha256. All four pass on all 634 variants."},"2597":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-1","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/use/sdk#nothing-to-clone","content":"Nothing to clone"},"2598":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-2","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/use/sdk#add-the-test","content":"Add the test"},"2599":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-3","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/use/sdk#go-further","content":"Go further"},"2600":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-4","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/use/sdk#coming-in-the-grant","content":"Coming in the grant"},"2601":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-5","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/use/sdk#join-the-matrix","content":"Join the matrix"},"2602":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-6","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk","content":"**What it is.** One test file for your SDK's test suite. It fetches every ledger in the corpus at a pinned commit, checks each one's sha256, decodes it as `LedgerCloseMeta`, encodes it again, and checks that the bytes are identical."},"2603":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-7","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk","content":"**Why it matters.** A ledger that does not round-trip is a ledger your SDK reads wrongly: a field dropped, a value changed, or a shape it cannot read at all. The corpus is the rarest valid ledgers stellar-core writes, so this is the cheapest way to meet them in CI before a user does."},"2604":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-8","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#nothing-to-clone","content":"The test reads `manifest.json` from GitHub at a pinned commit, then each ledger it lists from `raw.githubusercontent.com/XDRProof/xdrproof/<commit>/entries/<id>/<file>`, and checks every file against the sha256 the manifest records. It keeps what it fetched in `.xdrproof/`, so later runs read from disk: cache that folder in CI, and add it to `.gitignore`. To move to a newer corpus, change the commit."},"2605":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-9","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#nothing-to-clone","content":"The manifest lists  ledgers. The  mined from the public data lake ship zstd-compressed (`.xdr.zst`); the test decompresses them, and the recorded sha256 is of the ledger itself."},"2606":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-10","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#nothing-to-clone","content":"**Prefer a pinned copy in your repository?** Add it as a git submodule and point the test's cache folder at it (`CACHE = \"xdrproof\"`); it then reads the files in place and fetches nothing."},"2607":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-11","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#add-the-test","content":"`@stellar/stellar-sdk` 17.4.0. Node 22.15 or later, for its built-in `fetch` and zstd."},"2608":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-12","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#add-the-test","content":"From an empty folder, on Node 25.9: the first run fetches the corpus (98 seconds here, most of it the download); a second run reads `.xdrproof/` and takes under 5 seconds."},"2609":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-13","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#add-the-test","content":"`github.com/stellar/go-stellar-sdk` v0.9.0. `xdr.SafeUnmarshal` keeps the SDK's default depth limit."},"2610":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-14","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#add-the-test","content":"`go mod tidy` is needed: `go get` on the SDK's root module leaves the `xdr` package's own dependencies out of `go.sum`, and a first try without it stopped with `missing go.sum entry`. From an empty folder, on Go 1.25.14 (the official `golang` Docker image, as this machine has no Go installed):"},"2611":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-15","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#add-the-test","content":"`go test -v` lists 634 passing subtests, one per ledger, and no failures."},"2612":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-16","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#add-the-test","content":"`stellar-xdr` 30.0.0 has **no default depth limit**: you choose one in `Limits`. The deepest corpus ledgers need more than 500 levels (see how deep is too deep); 1500 reads them all."},"2613":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-17","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#add-the-test","content":"The types are at the crate root in 30.0.0 (a first try with `stellar_xdr::curr::…` did not compile). From an empty folder, on cargo 1.98.1:"},"2614":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-18","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#add-the-test","content":"`stellar-sdk` 17.0.1 (py-stellar-base), with `zstandard` for the mined ledgers."},"2615":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-19","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#add-the-test","content":"From an empty folder, on Python 3.13.13, fetching included:"},"2616":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-20","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#go-further","content":"A byte round trip is the floor. Each entry also lists the **facts** a correct reader must find (`expected.facts` in its `entry.json`, at a JSON Pointer path in the SEP-0051 rendering), and some list SDK checks (`expected.sdk_checks`). Conformance runs all of them, through the same bytes, in every build."},"2617":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-21","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#go-further","content":"The deepest valid ledgers in the corpus need more than 500 levels of XDR nesting. js-stellar-sdk 17.4.0, go-stellar-sdk v0.9.0 and py-stellar-base 17.0.1 read all of them with their defaults. rs-stellar-xdr has no default, so pick a limit (1500 above). `@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0 refuses  of them: F-1."},"2618":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-22","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#coming-in-the-grant","content":"These packages are what the grant funds. None exists today; the usage below shows the plan, and names and syntax may change at release."},"2619":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-23","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#coming-in-the-grant","content":"**Tranche 1, by 26 January 2027:** `xdrproof` on npm, the command-line tool (`verify`, `coverage`, `conformance`, `replay`, `guide check` and `check`), and `@xdrproof/corpus`, the corpus with `loadEntries({ hazards, caps, protocol })` and Vitest and Jest helpers."},"2620":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-24","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#coming-in-the-grant","content":"**Tranche 2, by 9 March 2027:** the GitHub Action `XDRProof/action`, which runs `xdrproof check` against a pinned corpus in your CI and uploads SARIF to code scanning; a Go module with the corpus embedded and a table-test helper; and a Rust crate that loads a corpus release."},"2621":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-25","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#join-the-matrix","content":"Conformance runs  SDK builds over the whole corpus today (JavaScript, WebAssembly, Rust, Go and two Python packages), plus  older releases on the fixed-bug regressions. Any SDK can join by wrapping itself in a **testee**: a small program that reads one JSON request per line on stdin and writes one JSON answer per line on stdout."},"2622":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-26","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#join-the-matrix","content":"| `op`    | Input | Answer when `ok`                                                 |\n| ------- | ----- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `hello` | none  | `implementation: {sdk, package, version, runtime}`, `ops: [...]` |","table":"table-0"},"2623":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-27","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#join-the-matrix","content":"| `op`     | Input                                                                    | Answer when `ok`                                                          |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `decode` | `{type, xdr}`: a type name as rs-stellar-xdr spells it, and base64 bytes | `xdr`: your re-encoding; `json`: your SEP-0051 rendering, if you have one |","table":"table-0"},"2624":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-28","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#join-the-matrix","content":"| `op`          | Input          | Answer when `ok` |\n| ------------- | -------------- | ---------------- |\n| `encode_json` | `{type, json}` | `xdr`: base64    |","table":"table-0"},"2625":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-29","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#join-the-matrix","content":"| `op`            | Input      | Answer when `ok`                                                                        |\n| --------------- | ---------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `strkey_decode` | `{strkey}` | `kind`: the first letter; `payload`: hex of the bytes between version byte and checksum |","table":"table-0"},"2626":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-30","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#join-the-matrix","content":"| `op`     | Input                  | Answer when `ok`                                     |\n| -------- | ---------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\n| `native` | `{type: \"ScVal\", xdr}` | `value`: your native projection, rendered losslessly |","table":"table-0"},"2627":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-31","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#join-the-matrix","content":"Every answer carries `status`: `ok`, `error` (with the stage and message) or `unsupported`. Anything your SDK cannot do is `unsupported`, recorded as not applicable, never as a failure. The six testees in `conformance/testees/` are working examples, from 28 to 118 lines each."},"2628":{"id":"/docs/use/sdk-32","page_id":"/docs/use/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/use/sdk#join-the-matrix","content":"The Java, Swift, Dart, PHP and .NET SDKs are not run today. Testees for all five, run over the whole corpus with every difference pinned, are Deliverable 10 of the grant's Tranche 3 (Architecture)."}},"count":2628},"sorting":{"language":"multilingual","sortableProperties":["content","page_id","type","url"],"sortablePropertiesWithTypes":{"content":"string","page_id":"string","type":"string","url":