 ledgers"],[2600,"Coming in the grant"],[4,"Compile it"],[2190,"Concepts"],[2304,"Conformance"],[2621,"Conformance runs  SDK builds over the whole corpus today (JavaScript, WebAssembly, Rust, Go and two Python packages), plus  older releases on the fixed-bug regressions. Any SDK can join by wrapping itself in a **testee**: a small program that reads one JSON request per line on stdin and writes one JSON answer per line on stdout."],[2131,"Conformance, for SDKs"],[1370,"Core applies a ledger's classic phase before its Soroban phase and re-checks the source account, sequence number, signatures and sequence-age preconditions at apply (TransactionFrame::commonValid;…"],[1377,"Core applies a ledger's classic phase before its Soroban phase and re-checks the source account, sequence number, signatures and sequence-age preconditions at apply (TransactionFrame::commonValid;…"],[1772,"Core applies a ledger's classic phase before its Soroban phase and re-checks the source account, sequence number, signatures and sequence-age preconditions at apply (TransactionFrame::commonValid;…"],[1779,"Core applies a ledger's classic phase before its Soroban phase and re-checks the source account, sequence number, signatures and sequence-age preconditions at apply (TransactionFrame::commonValid;…"],[1786,"Core applies a ledger's classic phase before its Soroban phase and re-checks the source account, sequence number, signatures and sequence-age preconditions at apply (TransactionFrame::commonValid;…"],[1835,"Core applies a ledger's classic phase before its Soroban phase and re-checks the source account, sequence number, signatures and sequence-age preconditions at apply (TransactionFrame::commonValid;…"],[2020,"Core re-checks sequence numbers, signatures and preconditions at apply. So a classic transaction earlier in the same ledger can make a queued Soroban transaction fail with `txBAD_AUTH_EXTRA` (a signature needed at submission becomes surplus once a master weight rises), and fee-bump inner transactions fail with `txBAD_SEQ`, `txNO_ACCOUNT`, `txBAD_AUTH`, `txBAD_AUTH_EXTRA` or `txBAD_MIN_SEQ_AGE_OR_GAP` inside `txFEE_BUMP_INNER_FAILED`. The corpus's failed-transaction family holds them."],[1334,"Core refuses a memo or muxed source only on InvokeHostFunction (TransactionFrame::validateSorobanMemo); on TTL and restore operations both are valid."],[1957,"Core's metadata stream is split into one `ledger-NNN.xdr` file per ledger, plus `SHA256SUMS`. An entry is one of those ledgers, chosen because it holds a rare shape."],[2470,"Corpus entries whose order varies pin an order-normalised hash, and list every raw ordering seen (`change_order` in the schema)."],[2334,"Coverage"],[517,"CREATE_ACCOUNT fails with CREATE_ACCOUNT_ALREADY_EXIST"],[524,"CREATE_ACCOUNT fails with CREATE_ACCOUNT_LOW_RESERVE"],[531,"CREATE_ACCOUNT fails with CREATE_ACCOUNT_UNDERFUNDED"],[265,"CREATE_ACCOUNT: the new account appears as a CREATED entry and as a CAP-0067 transfer event"],[538,"CREATE_CLAIMABLE_BALANCE fails with CREATE_CLAIMABLE_BALANCE_LOW_RESERVE"],[552,"CREATE_CLAIMABLE_BALANCE fails with CREATE_CLAIMABLE_BALANCE_NOT_AUTHORIZED"],[559,"CREATE_CLAIMABLE_BALANCE fails with CREATE_CLAIMABLE_BALANCE_UNDERFUNDED"],[194,"CREATE_CLAIMABLE_BALANCE with predicate and(not(before_relative 60), or(before_absolute 2100-01-01, unconditional))."],[229,"CREATE_CONTRACT (v1 host function) with a contract-ID preimage from the asset XPF: the instance's executable is stellar_asset, not a Wasm hash."],[222,"CREATE_CONTRACT_V2 from alice with salt sha256(\"xdrproof:kit:0\"): the contract ID is derived from the network ID, the deployer and the salt."],[301,"CREATE_CONTRACT_V2 whose executable is CONTRACT_EXECUTABLE_EXTERNAL_REF {owner CA1, tag \"kit\"}: the instance names no Wasm hash."],[201,"CREATE_CONTRACT_V2 with three constructor arguments (owner, tag, signers): the constructor runs inside the deploy and writes instance and persistent storage."],[1462,"CREATE_PASSIVE_SELL_OFFER returns a MANAGE_SELL_OFFER result"],[1469,"Deauthorizing a trustline removes offers, redeems pool shares into claimable balances and deletes the pool"],[2120,"Depth bombs, over-length vectors, truncated input and non-canonical encodings that an SDK must reject safely."],[2477,"Derive the probes"],[1946,"Deterministic scripts that drive a bare standalone stellar-core to emit rare shapes: fixed seeds, fixed close times, no RPC, no network, no funds."],[2134,"Distribution and CI"],[2314,"Each build runs as a **testee**: a small process that reads one JSON request per line on stdin and answers one JSON line on stdout. The runner (`conformance/run.mjs`) judges every answer against a reference and against that build's pre-registered expectations."],[2331,"Each build's `conformance/expectations/<testee>.json` lists every check it is known to fail, with the reason and the finding it belongs to. They are written from a measured run and reviewed by hand. After that the run must match them exactly: **an unexpected failure and an unexpected pass both fail it**, so a new bug and an upstream fix both show up."],[2583,"Each command gets the session in its environment: `XDRPROOF_FIRST_LEDGER`, `XDRPROOF_LAST_LEDGER`, `XDRPROOF_NETWORK_PASSPHRASE`, `XDRPROOF_RPC_URL` (and a `_DOCKER` form that reaches the host from a container), `XDRPROOF_TARGET_REF` and more; the full list is in `replay/README.md`."],[2172,"Each corpus ledger is served to a real indexer build through a mock stellar-rpc, in a fresh session with its own database. The verdict comes from what the indexer stored, never from its logs. An indexer joins with a small `adapter.toml` and a few shell commands; wallet-backend is the reference. On `main`,  of  variants are handled and  halt. Replay"],[2005,"Each finding says where it comes from: a committed measurement, a mined ledger, or an observation on recipe runs. The recipe runs themselves are not shipped. The recipes are, along with the order-scan and flag-profile results."],[2488,"Each fixed failure the corpus reproduces is replayed at the commits before its fix and at the fix (`replay/sightings.json`). Each must fail before and pass at the fix."],[2219,"Each interesting ledger becomes a corpus **entry**, compiled from a hand-written catalog file. An entry holds:"],[2586,"Each ledger gets two attempts: if the first does not get past it, the indexer is restarted once on the same store."],[2561,"Each line is `file:line:col: RULE: message`. The exit status is 0 with no findings, 1 with findings, and 2 when the packages could not be loaded. All four of these are real; what each one means, and the patches that clear them, are on Linter."],[2353,"Each pair agrees"],[2504,"Each probe names a fact by its key and states the expected value in a canonical form: strkeys, decimal strings, XDR enum names. A muxed `M…` account is named by its `G…` account."],[2557,"Each rule has sub-rules with stable IDs; the full list, with the evidence behind each, is on Linter."],[1964,"Each run starts the `stellar/quickstart` image pinned by digest in `recipes/core-image`, and refuses anything but stellar-core v30.0.0. The core config sets manual close, a fixed node seed, and one **emitter-flag profile** from `recipes/flags/`:"],[2173,"Each SDK build is wrapped in a small program that speaks one line-delimited JSON protocol, so a new language needs one small program. Every known difference is pinned with its cause, so a new failure and a fix upstream both fail the run until the expectation is updated. Conformance"],[2284,"Each target has its own page under Tools. `make all` runs verify, oracle, coverage, generate, conformance, replay, lint-selftest and lint."],[566,"END_SPONSORING_FUTURE_RESERVES fails with END_SPONSORING_FUTURE_RESERVES_NOT_SPONSORED"],[1960,"Entries are compiled from recipe steps (and mined ledgers) by the catalog:  so far. The hazard registry references recipe ledgers by number."],[1915,"Entries grow by purpose, not by volume. Each one names th