rpus"],[2433,"The corpus grows by purpose, not volume. Novelty checks that every entry earns its place. It describes each entry as a set of **atoms**:"],[1914,"The corpus is a set of **entries**, plus 37 mined ledgers from the real networks. Each entry is one rare shape, such as a signed-payload signer or a trustline created by a contract call, kept as real `LedgerCloseMeta` bytes. Each entry also carries the facts a correct reader must find in those bytes."],[2345,"The corpus rows are computed from the corpus at commit , the same way `make coverage` computes them. The goldens row is a fixed measurement at stellar-core commit `f2b91947`. The goldens are the ledgers stellar-core's own tests produce."],[2181,"The corpus, schema, tools and adapter contract are Apache-2.0 and run from a clone, with no XDRProof service in the path, so anyone can keep using them whatever happens to the maintainer. GitHub hosts the source, CI and releases; this site is generated from the repository. The tools collect no analytics. Milestone status is posted publicly in `STATUS.md` and weekly in #scf-general on the Stellar developer Discord."],[2525,"The create-contract authorization payload"],[1751,"The data entry is untouched; only its TTL entry, named by sha256 of the data's LedgerKey, changes."],[1660,"The declared refundable fee does not cover rent and events: INVOKE_HOST_FUNCTION_INSUFFICIENT_REFUNDABLE_FEE."],[137,"The deepest authorization tree core applies: 245 invocations; the WASM decoder rejects it"],[2617,"The deepest valid ledgers in the corpus need more than 500 levels of XDR nesting. js-stellar-sdk 17.4.0, go-stellar-sdk v0.9.0 and py-stellar-base 17.0.1 read all of them with their defaults. rs-stellar-xdr has no default, so pick a limit (1500 above). `@stellar/stellar-xdr-json` 30.0.0 refuses  of them: F-1."],[102,"The deploy succeeds and creates an instance; its auth root is the CreateContractV2 function with 244 nested never-called invocations."],[1723,"The deprecated but